WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:02:46.480
<v SPEAKER_1>My new book, Atlas of Unidentified Flying Objects, is now available to pre-order wherever books are sold, including Amazon, Barnes and Noble and Waterstones. Explore the UFO phenomenon through 34 cases from throughout the world, with accompanying pictures and maps in a beautiful hardback edition or download for your Kindle. Links available in the podcast description below. Hi everyone and welcome back to That UFO Podcast. As always, my name is Andy and this is part two of your Omnibus on The Authors. Folks who I have interviewed, who have written some sensational books in and around the topic of UFOs, of course. And this one's coming out to you with what I feel are some potential hidden gems that I've had over the years going back to 2022 and 2023. And again, it really surprises me how long ago some of these interviews were. For me, they seem like they happened last year. But all of them are 22 and 23. You're going to hear Kevin Randall discussing his book, Levelland, on the events of November 1957 in West Texas. A great interview from back in the day, I think that was 2022. Jeffrey Kripal, that's from 2023, talking UFOs and the impossible, talking about thinking impossibly and his work creating the archives of the impossible. A lot of impossible in there, but very much an enjoyable interview. And then one that I really feel went under the radar, mainly because of when it was released. So I recorded with Dr. Paul Thigpen on his book, Extra-terrestrial Intelligence and the Catholic Faith. I recorded this one, I think it was either Christmas Eve or the day after Christmas 2023. And it was released between Christmas and New Year. It's that time of year where people can take a bit of a break, especially from podcasting. You release some omnibus episodes like this one. But yeah, I really enjoyed the interview and hear some differing takes from a religious aspect, which I know is always a popular topic. Lots of great guests coming up in the next couple of weeks for fresh content, folks. But in the meantime, please enjoy The Authors Part 2. And don't forget, you can also check out my new book available for pre-order right now, as we record this in June 2025, Atlas of Unidentified Flying Objects. The genius link is in the description for the podcast. If you can pre-order, thank you so, so much. But anyway, over to the guests. Podcast, my name is Andy and delighted to welcome on to the show with me today, retired US. Air Force, author of over 100 books and UFO investigator, Kevin Randall. Kevin, welcome to the podcast.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.020
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, thank you very much, but I do need to make one quick correction. I'm retired Army officer.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.060
<v SPEAKER_1>Ah, my bad. Do you know what?

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.640
<v SPEAKER_2>Not really, not really, because I did spend some time in the Air Force as well.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:31.100
<v SPEAKER_1>So do you know what it is when I've seen USAF? I think I just automatically go into that on the book. But yes, thank you for correcting me, Hal. And I think I get away with it being British as well, don't I? That we're not as au fait with the American military as perhaps someone on the other side of the pond would be. But Kevin, you've got quite an interesting background, let alone what you've been involved in over the decades in terms of UFOs. What's your journey been like into ufology and UFOs? Was it something you had an interest in as a child or was it something that came much later?

00:03:31.100 --> 00:09:56.360
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, I always blame my mother for my interest. She was a fan of science fiction, which is about alien visitation and interstellar flight and alien civilizations and that sort of thing. And when I was a youngster, and this dates me, she took me to the movie Earth vs. the Flying Saucers. And that kind of sparked my interest because, of course, flying saucers were talking about alien civilizations and interstellar flight and alien visitation. So it's sort of the same thing, moving it from the realm of science fiction into the realm of reality. So that kind of sparked my interest. Back in the 1960s, when I got very interested in UFOs, the attitude was that, well, we just, they're just blobs of light in the distance. They're indistinct objects. They're things that can't really be identified because of those sorts of problems. And a friend of mine's mother had had a UFO sighting, flying saucer sighting. So I asked him if I could meet with her, and she, of course, agreed. And the only question I really wanted to answer was, was it a distinct object? And she said it was an object hovering about 200 feet over the barn. They lived on a ranch in South Dakota, if I remember correctly. And she said it had very distinct edges, very, very sharp, crisp edges. So it wasn't something seen in the distance. It wasn't a blob of light. It was a structured craft she had seen. And that sort of sparked my investigative nature as well, getting the answers for myself. And that, of course, has carried on through since that time. I've worked on enough, a lot of cases and an awful lot of in-field work, chased down an awful lot of sightings and interviewed an awful lot of witnesses to UFOs and that sort of thing. My military career, of course, spans decades as well. I started out as a helicopter pilot. I learned that the Army, the United States Army, was training high school graduates as helicopter pilots. And so I joined the Army to learn to become a helicopter pilot and was sent to Vietnam as a pilot at 19. Was an aircraft commander, spent a tour in Vietnam, came back, got out of the Army, went to college, got a degree and took ROTC. Now, the thing is, in the American military, those of us who were high school graduates and went through that program were appointed ward officers. So, we were the lowest ranking officer, but we weren't really commissioned, which made us eligible to take ROTC, the Reserve Officers Training Corps. So, while I was in college, I took the last two years of college in ROTC and was commissioned as an officer in the Air Force. So, I spent a number of years in the Air Forces in a number of different roles, mainly as an intelligence officer for them in a number of different places. Got out of the Army after 9-11, I joined the Iowa National Guard, which is an Army unit, as an intelligence officer, and eventually retired from that as a lieutenant colonel, having spent a tour in Iraq. So, I applied all that knowledge I gained as an intelligence officer to the investigations that I conducted specifically with Roswell. I was invited into the Roswell case by the Center for UFO Studies because they knew of my military background, and a lot of the witnesses that we would be talking to had military backgrounds or retired military officers. And they thought I'd be able to relate with them more and understand the lingo a little bit better than someone who had not served in the military. So, that kind of was the genesis of my invitation into the Roswell case with Don Schmidt. And we spent a great deal of time in Roswell, New Mexico, talking to witnesses, ferreting out new witnesses, and getting recordings, both video and audio recordings of a large number of witnesses, talked to some witnesses. I was the only one who talked to a couple of the witnesses, the way things worked out. Edwin Easley springs to mind, he was a provost marshal in Roswell in 1947. Provost marshal being like the chief of police on the air base, he was responsible for security and that sort of thing. And I talked to him a number of times. And the one thing that sticks in my mind talking to him, and some of it was recorded and some of it was not given the circumstances. But I was talking to him about the case, and I said, are we following the right path? And he said, what do you mean? And I said, well, we think it's extraterrestrial. And he said, let me put it this way, it's not the wrong path. So in essence, confirm the extraterrestrial nature of the Roswell case. And we had a number of officers, high-ranking officers in Roswell in 1947, confirmed that sort of an event, the single exception being one of the big guy named Barrelclaw, who is a retired colonel. And he said, no, nothing like that happened. He's the only member of General Ramey's staff that we were, Colonel Ramey, I'm sorry, Colonel Blanchard's staff, that we were able to talk to, who denied anything happened. All the others that we talked to said, yeah, it was something extraterrestrial. And this goes from the highest-ranking officers on the base down to the lowest-ranking enlisted soldiers on the base, that we were able to talk to privates and corporals and buck sergeants who said essentially the same thing. So we had a wide range of that sort of thing. So that kind of gives you a background of my interest in UFOs. And from there, of course, it expanded. And I was doing a number of books. I've been writing UFO articles. And I say I went to college on what I called the Saga Scholarship. Saga was a men's magazine, not like Playboy or Penthouse, but it just was articles that catered to men. And there might be a scantily clad picture of a woman in a magazine once in a while, but it really didn't, that wasn't the focus. It was the articles geared toward men. And they paid enough money. I was able to write an article of semester for the magazine and get paid for it, and that paid my tuition except for $10. So I say I went to college on the Saga scholarship. And I collected the magazine articles into a book later on in 1989 and added some new material to that. And then the second UFO book I did was on Roswell. And from there, it's expanded out. I've done 25 or 28 UFO books. So I've done a lot of research into it, and I understand what's going on in the world of UFOs.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:21.340
<v SPEAKER_1>That's an incredible, thanks for the backstory, because it's a wonderful history. You've put into just a couple of minutes. They are almost six or seven decades. After all that time, what is still driving you to write about the subject of UFOs? Because there's so many in this topic in a far shorter time, become disillusioned or they fall out of love with it. But you've kept that passion to keep writing even now?

00:10:21.360 --> 00:12:41.440
<v SPEAKER_2>One of my goals in life was to be a writer. And you mentioned I've published, I lost count of 125 books. It's somewhere over that number. But it's science fiction, it's action adventure and it's UFOs. And so I've always wanted to be a writer. So this is one way I could express my opinions that way. But the subject always fascinated me as well. And I had a good friend, Wilson Bob Tucker, and those who are into science fiction and science fiction conventions would know Bob Tucker. Wrote a number of science fiction books, really good science fiction books, but never got the kind of publicity that Isaac Asimov or Ray Bradbury got, but an incredible writer. And with that sort of thing going on, we wanted to, you know, well, the point was that at one point, talking to Bob Tucker is that he explained that he was retired from writing. And as a much younger person, I couldn't understand that, why you would retire from writing. And here I am now at an age commensurate with what he was at the time. And I'm still writing. I have a book due to Philip Mantle called Understanding Roswell. Actually, it was due a number of weeks ago. I'm a little late on it. But it's almost finished now and will be in time for the 75th anniversary. But it's just sort of a drive. I just feel the urge to continue writing. And because in the last year or so, actually the last six months, I've had to do three UFO books. I've been requested to do the books. The Levelland Sighting, which we'll talk about, I guess a little later, is what I think of as the second most important UFO sighting. Because of the number of people involved in the interactions with the environment. But it's just a story that I wanted to tell. And I think that's the whole point. I continue to want to tell the story. So I just can't see myself retiring. I can see myself slowing down. And for publishing fewer books, but I just can't see a retirement from. So it's kind of the desire to tell the stories, to engage people in things that interest me and continue the research into the UFO phenomenon until we get a definitive answer.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:58.620
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, that passion and drive is taking you to write about Levelland, which we're of course going to talk about. And I'd like to do that now. The events of Levelland, West Texas, I believe, it was November 1957. Can you tell us a little bit, if you remember, when you first heard about this particular event?

00:12:59.880 --> 00:18:58.780
<v SPEAKER_2>It's always been an interest of mine because of the nature of the sightings. It was people at a number of different locations, independently reporting to the sheriff. This thing had come close to the ground or landed on the ground near the car, installed the engine and put out the headlights and that sort of thing. And looking at that whole group of sightings in November of 1957. So I've been interested in it for a long time. It was only recently that I began to understand the importance of it. A fellow named Don Berlinson, who lives in Roswell. Roswell being three hours by car from Levelland. Levelland, for those of you who want to look on a map, is right near Lubbock, Texas, in the Panhandle of Texas. So if you can't find Levelland, just go a little bit west of Lubbock, and that's where Levelland is. Had interviewed the widow of the sheriff back around the year 2000. And she told him some things that were very interesting to me and kind of inspired me to look a little bit deeper into it. One of the things I discovered is if you look at the Air Force file, the Project Lubbock file on Levelland, they interview the sheriff whose name is Weir Clem, a name I positively hate. I wish it was something like Tom Collins or Jim Beam for that matter, or James Bond, some good name, not Weir Clem. But he had told the Air Force according to their file, well, he eventually was interested enough because there had been enough sightings been reported to him that they were going to go out and look for it. And he saw the object in the distance. He said it was just to streak a light in sight for two seconds. That's what it says in the Air Force file. I found documentation printed in newspapers prior to the Air Force investigation, where he was talking about seeing a oval-shaped object that was bright red. And then later on, a fellow named Don Berliner, yes, there's a lot of dons in this conversation. Don Berliner in the mid-1970s talked to the sheriff, talked to Sheriff Clem. And Clem again said that he had seen the object. So we've got him saying it prior to the Air Force investigation and then somewhat later after the Air Force had written down what he had to say. The other thing that came out, and we're back to Don Berliner now, he learned that the mechanic for the sheriff's department was still alive in 2000 and interviewed him and said that the sheriff had come into the shop and wanted his car checked out the next day. The only reason the sheriff would have had his car checked out the next day is if he'd gotten close enough to the object that it stalled it. So we have documentation saying get close enough to it to see an object. Then we have information that suggests his car was stalled. Now, what makes this even more important in some of the other sightings of in that short time frame of two and a half hours in Levelland, is that he was in a mini convoy. He was in one of the sheriff's department car with a deputy. Behind him was a state police car. I think then it was called the Texas Department of Public Safety. That's what it was when I lived there in the 1960s, and I think it was that in 1957. So now you have state police involved. And behind them was a third car with Air Force officers in it. This tells me that they got close enough that their cars were stalled. And that tells me the Air Force officers observed the phenomenon of the car engines being stalled and the bright red glowing object being close enough that they could see what it was. But there's nothing in the Air Force file that talks about an investigation or interrogation of the Air Force officers involved. All we have are six different interviews conducted two or three days later by a staff sergeant that came down from Colorado Springs and interviewed the sheriff, a couple of the witnesses and let it go at that. Didn't do a comprehensive investigation. That was it. Now, years later, many years later, I was able to find the names of many, many people who were involved based not only on what the Air Force report said, the Air Force documents say, but the newspaper files that we could go through. And we'd find witnesses in there that were interviewed. The APRO Bulletin, the Aerial Phenomenon Research Organization, which was a civilian organization, was involved in the investigation in 1957, as was the National Investigations Committee on Aerial Phenomena, which is also a civilian organization based on Washington, DC. They had one of their investigators out there talking to people. So looking at all these documents created at that time, we were able to find the names of many, many other people who were involved. The sheriff said that he had received dozens of phone calls about this, which suggests additional witnesses as well. So it's a case with many, many witnesses at independent locations reporting the same phenomenon. The sheriff also, at least according to the wife, the widow, said that there had been landing traces on a ranch outside of town. I think it was north of town. Berlinson went there and interviewed the widow of the rancher, but the rancher's daughter as well. And the rancher and the daughter went out and saw the burned area, as did the sheriff. So now we have landing traces on the ground, which had they been researched properly in 1957, might have presented us with some very interesting data. We know that it interacted with the environment by stalling the car engines and filling radios with static and putting out the headlights. And when the object left, left and the cars could be restarted, and it was only one case where the guy said the car started spontaneously, but the cars could be started. So they stopped working at the close approach of the UFO and began working again when the UFO was gone. So we've got an interaction with the environment and we have many independent witnesses. Think of the data we could have collected at that time, had NICAP not got involved with the Air Force in an argument over the number of witnesses. So the Air Force successfully diverted the conversation from what happened to how many witnesses there really were.

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:18.480
<v SPEAKER_1>Now, am I right and correct me here that Dr. Alan Hynek was involved, and this was before Blue Book was official. It was Project Sign. He was a consultant at the time, and he was involved in explaining the event as being prosaic. But if I'm correct, he wasn't actually involved in the investigation and had only seen that Air Force report.

00:19:18.480 --> 00:21:43.580
<v SPEAKER_2>Hynek was not involved directly with the Levelland case. Hynek came on board as a scientific consultant to, I think, Project Sign, as you said, which was in 1948. And he continued in that mission almost through the end of Project Blue Book, the 22 years from Sign to Grudge to Blue Book. But he had nothing really to do with Levelland. The investigator, the official investigator was a staff sergeant, Norman Barth, who came out of an air intelligence service squadron in Colorado Springs and flew down to Reese Air Force Base, Reese Air Force Base being in Lubbock. And then it's just a short drive to Levelland from there. So they didn't spend a lot of money on the investigation. But the official conclusion, which is still registered in the Project Blue Book files today in the Master Index, it was created, is it was Ball Lightning. Well, anybody who knows anything about Ball Lightning knows that there's still an argument about whether there is such a thing as Ball Lightning. But more importantly, the descriptions of it is something that's very small, 18 inches, two feet in diameter and very short live, a matter of seconds. So it's a glowing ball of blue fire, so to speak, that winks out of existence rapidly. We're talking about sightings of something was bright red for the most part, Pedro Saucido, who is the first person to call the Levelland Sheriff, at I think 1030 that night on November 2nd, said it was a brightly glowing blue object that landed close to his truck, stalled the truck, he was so frightened he dived out of the truck. The passenger was even more petrified and sat in the passenger's seat while the object sat on the ground and then it turned to a bright, glowing red and it took off and then the truck could be restarted. So we have the descriptions of the color changes, but for the most part, people were talking about a bright red object on the ground that was in sight for minutes. The whole series of sightings in Levelland took place over about two, two and a half hours on November 2nd into the morning of November 3rd. I think one of the witnesses talked about sitting there watching it for 15 minutes. Another said he watched it for five minutes. So no matter how you look at it, ball lightning is not a credible answer for what happened in Roswell. I'm sorry, in Levelland. Roswell just pops into my mind because I'm finished the book on Roswell now.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:57.600
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, I was going to ask on that, is there anything particularly special about Levelland in your research, in your investigations, that this event happened here? Was there anything nearby or?

00:21:58.000 --> 00:25:34.060
<v SPEAKER_2>Nothing. With Roswell, you can talk about the Atomic Strike Force being based at Roswell. And you can look at New Mexico where not that far from Roswell, we're firing rockets into the atmosphere higher than ever before. And now, of course, we've gone to the moon. But if you're interstellar race, if you're a space traveling race and you find a civilization that's on the verge of taking its first steps into space and they have atomic weapons, you're going to be concerned about those people. But Levelland, no, there's nothing special about it other than it's three hours from Roswell. Now, an hour or so after the sightings ended in Levelland, there were sightings at the White Sands Missile Range by military police. The Air Force wrote those off as being hysteria and misidentification of the moon because of the Levelland sightings. But the first sightings were by a couple of MPs within hours of the sightings in Levelland. They talked about something that came down close to the ground. They could see it silhouetted against the mountains behind the object. So clearly, it wasn't the moon, it wasn't Venus, as the Air Force claimed. It was something much closer and something was much closer to the ground. And the Air Force dismissed it and said, well, the MPs were very young, they were 20, 21 years old. And they got caught up in the hysteria of the time. And I'm thinking, no, they couldn't have been caught up in the hysteria of the time because there wasn't social media. There wasn't immediate access to this information about the sightings in Levelland as they were happening, as we'd have today. You'd have everybody on their cell phones saying, geez, you know, come out here, look what's going on. It didn't get reported until the next morning. So they had they had no knowledge of this. They couldn't been caught up in the hysteria. I talked to one of the MPs. And he just he told me exactly what they'd seen. And of course, that's recorded in the book, Levelland. But the thing that really annoyed me was the dismissal of them saying, well, they were poorly trained and they were young men and they just really didn't know what was going on. And I'm thinking, yeah, they're 20, 21 years old. I think I think Glenn Toy, who was the one I talked to, I think he was 21 in 1957. And I'm thinking, I'm in Vietnam as a helicopter pilot and an aircraft commander at 19. I think George HW. Bush was a pilot in the Navy at 19 during the Second World War. So a great deal of responsibility is placed on the shoulders of very young individuals. And there's not a second thought about that. The things always cracked me up. We had one of the helicopter pilots in the flights. We called him Papa because he was the oldest pilot flying with us. He was 23. And so we're thinking, the oldest guy is 23. So we were all 20, 21, 22 years old. I think I was the youngest pilot in the unit at the time. But the dismissal of the testimony simply because they were young men is just preposterous. And that's kind of the attitude of the Air Force. What can we do to dismiss the cases or make it look like it's something that it's not? And we see that time after time after time when you look at the Air Force files and you go through the files and you say, you know, this sighting really isn't identified as ball lightning or Venus coming down and landing 150 feet away from the guys in the jeep on White Sands. So we take a look at all of that sort of thing.

00:25:34.060 --> 00:26:06.240
<v SPEAKER_1>Now, like you say, you've got multiple sightings happening over hours and over quite a large space as well. That's always a good thing when it comes to data. And for a researcher and an investigator, you can actually say, well, they saw it here and they saw it here. Then they saw this over here as well. Is that something that you have managed to see that there could be any prosaic explanation for? Is there something you've looked at and thought, well, possibly it could be this, but ultimately you're obviously leaning towards some more ET explanation.

00:26:06.240 --> 00:31:47.720
<v SPEAKER_2>With Levelland, you've got it interacting with the environment, stalling the car engines. So what kind of natural phenomenon or mechanism do we have in 1957 that would do that? In my research, I found that the Japanese during the Second World War were experimenting with magnetic effects and seeing if they could stop an engine with a magnetic ray, if they could get close enough to it, because if you can knock the other guy's fighter planes out of the sky with a magnetic ray from a distance your way ahead, I think they managed to get it to work up to 10 feet. They just could not get beyond, I guess, the universe square a lot, which is the farther away you get, you reduce it by a square as opposed to doubling it. But they couldn't get beyond that. So we know of no mechanism that we have, and I don't think there's anything in the technology today that we have that would do that. And it would be interesting to say, well, you know, it was just something wrong with the car. In fact, the Air Force pulled that out in Levelland. Pedro Saucido had his truck prepared, I think the day before the sightings. And after the sightings, it took it back in to have it looked at, and they found a piece of metal in the rotor, which could have shorted out the, in the distributor, could have shorted out the firing of the spark plugs. But then you have to ask yourself, well, why did it only manifest itself once? And why only when this object was close by and once the object was gone, well, then I can start my truck again. And then you have all the others that report the same thing. So we know of no mechanism. The Air Force, I think, you mentioned Hynek, and I think Hynek at once, once had said about the Levelland sightings, well, you can, you can eliminate the Pedro C. Cito sighting. And I think, well, no, you really can't. He said the other one would be a coincidence. But when you get the two coincidences, then you're moving it pretty much outside the realm of coincidence. And then when we begin talking about people identified at 13 separate locations at least that we know of, then you've moved it way beyond coincidence. So there is no mechanism that explains the sightings in terrestrial fashion. And I think that's an important point with the Levelland sightings is we have no terrestrial explanation that accounts for all the reported malfunctions of vehicles and the sightings of the object. I mean, it's a glowing red. I think Saucido called it a torpedo shaped. Other people's called it egg shaped. And I'm thinking, you know, that's probably a matter of perspective where you were when you saw the object, how you looked at it. If you look at, you know, like a tanker truck for straight on, it looks like a big cigar shaped object. But if you look at it from an angle, then you begin to get egg shaped or other looking shapes to it. So it's a matter of perspective, not the craft changing shape, but it's just a matter of perspective. And we have an awful lot of discussion of what it looked like and how long it was visible and what it did when it was on the ground. Now, if we could have coupled it to the landing trace case, then we've got an even greater body of evidence about it. So we had an opportunity there to investigate this case properly. But the Air Force at that time was more interested in explaining cases and keeping the public misinformed about UFOs than it was in actually investigating UFOs. Two or three days later, on November 6th, I think it was a guy named James Stokes was driving from Alamogordo, the Hullman Air Force Base to El Paso, Texas. And if you look at your map, it's what an hour and a half, two hours from Alamogordo to El Paso. You go through a little town called Oro Grande. And as he approached Oro Grande, his radio began to fade out. And it was when it was going on, then his engine sputtered and quit and he pulled over the side of the road. And he's sitting there behind six or seven other cars. And they're watching an H-shaped object as it flies around overhead for a moment. When the object disappeared, of course, he got into his car. And I don't know if he continued on to El Paso and then went home that night. But when he got home to Alamogordo, they called Jim Lorenzen, who was with Corl Lorenzen, the leader of the Aero Phenomenon Research Organization. They were based in Alamogordo at the time. And he went to see them and they noticed he had a slight, like sunburn on half of his face and one of his arms. And if you remember close encounters at the third kind, Richard Dreyfus had that similar problem, much more pronounced from reading it, much more pronounced than what Stokes experienced. But here's another group of sightings with multiple witnesses again and multiple car stories. And another effect of the UFO. And the Air Force spent a lot of time maligning James Stokes, who was an engineer working at Hullman Air Force Base. And his bosses at the base were saying, well, he's an engineer working for us. He spent 20 years in the Navy and he doesn't have a, he wasn't a licensed engineer, but he was doing engineering stuff. And his bosses are saying, yes, he's an engineer. And the Air Force that came in out was, well, we couldn't find a degree for him for Air Force, so we can't trust what he has to say because he's embellishing his resume. He's working as an engineer. Whether he's got a degree or not is irrelevant. He's got a position where he is called an engineer on an Air Force base. So you can see how they tried to belittle the witnesses and reduce their importance and their credibility by smearing their reputations. And you can see in many other cases, them doing the same things with other witnesses, doing everything they can to make the person look like an egotist or somebody who's jumping on the bandwagon or somebody who's not very bright. And you get into the depth of the person and you learn there's a great deal more to them than just the smear thrown out by the Air Force.

00:31:47.720 --> 00:32:14.220
<v SPEAKER_1>Kevin, you read my mind when you brought up the effects, the physical effects of close contact, because that was going to be my next question. So it's interesting you had that, you say sunburn effect, so these objects were clearly within a range or causing some sort of interference that was affecting the human skin. Was there any lasting effects you found out from people that maybe lasted a lot longer into later life? Was there any cancers or rare diseases?

00:32:14.600 --> 00:35:18.720
<v SPEAKER_2>In Levelland, no, the sunburn effect on Stokes faded in a matter of hours. In fact, when the Air Force showed up to investigate a couple of days later, his skin tone had returned to normal. There was no evidence of the reddening of the sunburn effect. There's another story that one of the MPs was not available for the Air Force investigation. I think his name was Will Banks. And the story is he was in the hospital. But what they told the Air Force was that we gave him a three-day pass. And I'm thinking, yeah, we know that there's going to be an investigation. We know the guy is coming down. And now we put him on a three-day pass. Now if you're on a three-day pass, and I know this from my military experience, you have to leave a phone number. You have to be, they have to be able to contact you. So even if the person who granted the three-day pass didn't know the Air Force was coming down to investigate, he would have learned when they got there and he would have been recalled Will Banks, I believe it's Will Banks, to the Holloman Air Force base so he could be interviewed. The other thing is a three-day pass has limitation of how far you can travel. He would have been basically in the local area. The story is from the Lorenzins is he was in the hospital, and he was in the hospital because of the effects, but there was no lasting effects. We move into the realm of the Cash Landrum sighting in 1980, where there were the apparent lasting effects. John Schuchler, I think it is, has done a great deal of work investigating that case. Kirk Collins on his blog has a great deal of information about the Cash Landrum case. If you go to your search engine, you just type in Cash Landrum, I think it'll take you to all the information that's available on this. Philip Glass said that the effects experienced by the two women and the young boy were pre-existing conditions. But it doesn't seem that's quite accurate because it seems that some of the later effects, and I think there may have been some cancer involved in there. I'm not sure because I haven't spent a lot of time investigating that case. But I think there was some cancer effects there and were lasting throughout their lives. There have been cases where people have suffered more debilitating effects than was exhibited in Levelland. So we have a range of those sorts of things. And that's the other thing. There's all these subsets of ufology, and I'm chasing one now where people were trying to take pictures or video with their cell phones of UFOs and the camera failed. Camera worked fine before the UFO was there. Camera worked fine after the UFO was gone, but when they were trying to video the UFO, camera didn't work. Sometimes it gets sound. You can hear the sound. You can hear what they're saying, and you can hear all the sound in the background. But the image of the UFO is gone and that sort of thing. And so I've been looking into those cases. There are quite a few of those where the cameras have failed on that. So that's another kind of side effect of a close approach of the UFO.

00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:49.060
<v SPEAKER_1>One event that I suppose we were just before camera phones became mass-manufactured or even available was the Phoenix Lights, of course. And that's the kind of thing we would have loved to have seen more footage from. There was a huge fallout from the Phoenix Lights event in the mid-90s because so many people in the city saw these lights. They saw what they reported as a huge triangle or V-shape flying overhead. Was there any similar fallout from Levelland where the people of the town were scared? Did they report it? Was there anything that kind of carried on afterwards?

00:35:50.520 --> 00:40:10.400
<v SPEAKER_2>There doesn't seem to be, and I've looked at an awful lot of the newspapers from that time frame and you're looking at it, it seemed to be more of an interest in the subject. There doesn't seem to be anybody really frightened by it. Those who were experienced in their car stalling, they were frightened because they didn't understand what was going on. But for the people in the area around that, in Levelland, in Lubbock, in the West Texas, in the Panhandle, there doesn't seem to be any long lasting fright. Since you brought that up, I wonder if that might be a result of the Lubbock Lights, which was Lubbock, of course, right there in my Levelland. It took place in 1951. They had some experience with these sorts of phenomenon being reported and there's no lasting effect to anybody. I think they looked at it more in the realm of, not this kind of thing again, as opposed to, oh my God, what's happening around here. And I think as we move into our timeframe, when we're all experienced with movies and science fiction of alien visitation and that sort of thing, there's an awful lot of documentaries on UFOs. And I think a lot of people realize, this stuff's been going on, if we count from 1947 for 75 years, if we take it back to the Second World War where the Foo Fighters and the Ghost Rockets in Scandinavia and Northern Europe in 1946, it's been going on even longer. And there's really been no adverse effect. You can point to very few people who were injured by close approaches of UFOs, and that's usually a side effect, not something that is done purposely. We can look at the Thomas Mantell case from January of 1948. He was killed chasing a UFO. I believe now, based on the documentation, we've been able to secure over the years, and I'm at odds with a number of my colleagues in the field. I believe what he saw was a skyhook balloon. And I believe it was launched in Minnesota a day or so earlier. And if you watch the wind patterns, it would have been pretty much where Mantell saw it. And the problem wasn't that the UFO, whatever it might have been, took any action to stop Mantell. But Mantell violated one of the regulations. He climbed too high without benefit of oxygen. And I did some research into both anoxia and hypoxia, which one is the complete lack of oxygen in the blood, and the other was a reduced oxygen in blood, which has all kinds of effects on the brain. And I think at 24,000 feet without supplemental oxygen, the average person has a useful consciousness of about 10 minutes. If you get about 2,000 feet above that, you've got a useful consciousness of about four minutes. And if you get to 29,000 feet, it's like 29 seconds. But the problem is Mantell didn't climb directly to 25,000 feet or whatever. He did it in gradual stages. So he was up, they were circling at about 20,000 feet at one point. And the wingmen with Mantell started to experience, or one of them did start to experience the hypoxia. And they broke off the intercept. And Mantell says, well, I'm going to go to 25,000 feet and circle for 10 minutes. And if I can't get any closer, then I'll break it off. He didn't make it to 25,000 feet. He passed out with the aircraft timed to, trimmed to climb. And at about 30,000 feet, the torque of the engine pulled it over into a power dive and it disintegrated. The aircraft disintegrated about 19,000 feet based on the stresses on the airframe that it was not designed to withstand. So he was, if I am correct, he was killed chasing a skyhook balloon. If my colleagues are correct, he was killed chasing a flying saucer, but the flying saucer, the UFO took no action against him. So no matter what the object was, the result of the aircraft accident was Mantell violation of orders, which said above 14,000 feet you're required to use oxygen. And he didn't, although the aircraft was equipped for oxygen, the oxygen had not been charged and he may not have had his oxygen mask with him because the flight was scheduled to be at 5,000 feet. So there was no need for the oxygen equipment.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:45.500
<v SPEAKER_1>I have a couple of colleagues, Dave Partridge and Graham Rendell, who actually want to speak to you. But they are specialists from the UK, especially when it comes to historical UFO cases. Graham's got a fantastic book out about the Foo Fighters that you mentioned of World War II. But I'll pass that information on to you at the end, but they would love to speak to you about that Mantell case as well. What I would like to know, Kevin, is what can these historical events like Levelland, like Roswell that you've got a new book coming out about, you say as well, what can they teach us about the phenomenon today and help us understand what may be happening?

00:40:46.680 --> 00:45:58.200
<v SPEAKER_2>What we're looking at is investigations that took place at the time. So information was gathered at the time, and then we've got subsequent investigations conducted by both military and civilians through the years. So we've gathered a great deal of data. And what we see is an effort to suppress the information by the American government, and I think maybe the British government and other governments around the world to suppress the information. And I know the Air Force, the Australians were going to investigate UFOs based on reading Don Kehoe's book, Flying Saucers from Outer Space, or one of those books. And so the Air Force officers contacted American officers about UFOs, Flying Saucers, and the American's attitude was, oh, there's nothing to it, you can't believe a thing Kehoe says, don't do it. Turns out if you go back and you research Kehoe's book carefully, you see that most of what he printed, although he couldn't name all the sources he had because they were talking to him off the record, so to speak, the information was accurate. You see our Air Force, the US Air Force manipulating the Air Forces in other countries to not do solid investigations. But by going back and studying these cases, we learn an awful lot about not only what was going on, we get some clues about propulsion, we get some clues about other aspects of it. But the thing that's really important, I think, is if you're told repeatedly something is impossible, you probably don't attempt it. But if you realize it is possible and you're not sure how it's done, you begin to try to figure out how to do it yourselves. So if we are blessed with the information that interstellar flight is possible, we see it as impossible given the distances and the technology we possess at the time. We just can't really do it. But if somebody else has figured out how to do it, that means there's a means to it. And then we begin looking for the means rather than not looking whatsoever. And I think that's one of the important things that comes out of that, is learning something new about that. It tells us that we're not alone in the universe, but it also suggests a way that we may be able to explore part of our galaxy ourselves. And I think that's one of the important things to come out of it. And we get hints about it. We learn that the Air Force at one time was going to set up spectroscopic cameras around the country to see if they could photograph the light coming from UFOs and get a spectroscopic analysis. I'm not sure exactly what that would have revealed to them, but it might have given them a hint about the propulsion system. So, you know, something like that. Or we got a glowing object, the metallic structure of the craft. I don't know what all it would have told us. If they had the cameras out and located, we might have gotten photographs of the object independently from two or three locations, and that would tell us a great deal about its capabilities. How high was it flying? How fast it was going? How large was it? All those sorts of things we could have divined from a proper investigation. You can see in the Project Blue Book files, as we look through those, these sorts of things were attempted, but only haphazardly. They were going to put out some cameras, but well, we got one camera, so that's not much. Ed Rupelt, who was the Chief of Project Blue Book at one time, talked about the green fireballs which were seen over the desert southwest in great numbers in the late 1940s, and they were going to set up cameras to photograph the green fireballs. Apparently, they did set up a camera, and apparently they got pictures because there's hints to it, but the pictures never show up. We don't know where the pictures went, we don't know who has those pictures, we don't know where they went. If we do have a crashed UFO, as Roswell seems to suggest, and we can figure out how it works, then we take a big step over our competitors in the world, but we also learn how to travel interstellar distances. And I think the technology may be so far beyond our capability to understand at this point, we haven't figured it out. And the best example, it worked much better in the 1990s, is if you take a VCR, a power pack, and a TV set back to Merlin the Magician, and you show him the videotape, which is basically a black ribbon. And if you know how to decode it, show him how you put it in the machine, and you can see pictures and sound, the color pictures and sound. To duplicate that, he's got to understand two things that are invisible, electricity and magnetism. And he just did not have the cultural elements to understand that in the time of Merlin the Magician. And I think we may be at that point, we're presented with this technology, we do not have the technological ability to understand what all we've been presented with. I think we continue to work on it, and as our technology evolves, we apply it. But I don't think we figured much of it out yet.

00:45:58.200 --> 00:46:05.820
<v SPEAKER_1>People who listen to the podcast will appreciate, I like a good analogy. So I like that Merlin one. I'll probably steal that going forward at times, Kevin, if you don't mind.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:09.000
<v SPEAKER_2>Just credit me with it.

00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:33.980
<v SPEAKER_1>Always give credit, always will. And if I don't, people can pull me up on that. You've mentioned there obviously some common factors. Is there anything you've noticed in your years of writing, investigation and research that have given you any clues as to the craft's origin? You've mentioned interstellar travel, people potentially visiting from different galaxies. What, for you, is your best summary you can put together of what this phenomenon may be?

00:46:33.980 --> 00:50:38.860
<v SPEAKER_2>I would say it's not interplanetary, meaning it doesn't come from inside the solar system, because we pretty well surveyed all the bodies in the solar system that could have intelligent life on it producing these sorts of things. And we realize that Jupiter and Saturn are impossible, the same with Uranus and Neptune. Pluto is way out there in the cold world. There's really nothing apparently on Mars. Venus, you land your spacecraft on Venus and it's going to melt. And Mercury is kind of out of the question. Some of the moons around the planets, a Titan, for example, around the site, Saturn, would be more Earth-like. But then you've got a problem with the temperatures and things like that. So I think we pretty well ruled out interplanetary flight. And I think in the 1950s, the late 1940s, 1950s, they were thinking in terms of interplanetary flight as opposed to interstellar. When we move beyond that, I would guess, and it's only a guess, that they would come from within our galactic neighborhood. A hundred light-years away, fifty light-years away. I don't know how fast they can travel. I don't know how they would maneuver. They may be able to cross the galaxy in a matter of hours, based on their technology or a way of warping space to shorten those distances. I don't know, and if I had an answer to that, I'd be really, really rich, and I wouldn't be doing podcasts. I'd be on my mega-yacht, having a good time somewhere, or flying around in my private aircraft, flipping off people. I don't know. But I mean, the point is, I really don't know. We used to say that the only real clue we had was from the Betty Hill star map from her and Barney Hill's abduction in 1961. And she produced this star map. And Marjorie Fish created a 3D model and pegged it as being zeta 1, zeta 2 reticuli, which is a double star system sum, 37.5 light years from Earth. But that information is flawed because according to Betty Hill, it was a 3D representation she saw and she was drawing it in two dimensions, so that would have distorted it. Marjorie Fish, when she did her work, eliminated all red dwarf stars because she deduced there would be nothing around a red dwarf that would be of interest to a spacefaring race. And my counter argument would be that there may not be any civilized planets around that, but there may be some element that is crucial for them that they could find in one or two of these systems. So it may be important for them to look at red dwarfs. I don't know. There has been some work done suggesting better distances to those planet or those star systems that Marjorie Fish identified, are not where she thought they were. So the star map is kind of distorted there, but that was the only clue we really had. If you remember the movie Alien, the star system they go into is Zeta 1, Zeta 2 Reticuli. They mentioned that. I caught that some years later as I was watching the movie. I said, oh my God, they're there. That's where they found the alien. But I don't think that's relevant. But I really don't know. I just can't say, I would say that they come from inside our galaxy. Our galaxy is what, 110,000 light years across and 30,000 light years deep or something like that. We're on the outer edges, the fringes of the galaxy. So you could be talking about somebody who's 100,000 light years away. Then you move to other galaxies. I think the nearest galaxy is Andromeda, and it's what, 2.5 million light years away. So I can't see them traversing that distance. But if there's a way of bending space, maybe it is all things are possible. I don't know. So I don't know where they're coming. That's a long-winded answer to say, I don't know.

00:50:38.860 --> 00:51:43.080
<v SPEAKER_1>No, that's fair enough. But do you know what gives me a little bit of hope is, like you say, when you think about it seems impossible, and it's incomprehensible that we could traverse that distance, that level of space. But like you say, when Merlin saw the videotape, and saw that black ribbon, and then saw pictures on a screen, that would have seemed impossible as well. So there just might be that something that one day is really common that we don't quite understand yet. What I would like to put to you, Kevin, just before we get to some listener questions, I heard you on on Herbert Hughes' Unexplained radio show a couple of weeks ago. And you said there's been a lot of progress people have talked about that has been made in recent years, and some folks are getting really excited. We're going to get some form of disclosure from the government, something like that. But you said, like others have, that you've seen this before, you've experienced this, that it's potentially going to just fizzle away. The government are going to keep things classified. They're not going to get the reports that are due. Is there anything you have seen that you think maybe this time something could or maybe a little bit different that we get some form of progress on this subject?

00:51:44.500 --> 00:56:12.680
<v SPEAKER_2>You have to look at it from the point of view of the deep state, and you have to look at from the point view of the purpose of the deep state is to keep the deep state in power and keep those in those positions of power well-financed. They have no motivation to release the information. There's absolutely nothing they gain by releasing the information, and in fact, it could undercut their power. I did a book called UFOs in the deep state that came out what a year ago, something like that, and looked at this in depth, and people say, well, why do they continue to keep it classified? If you look at the way, at least our government works here in the United States, you have a transition of power at the very top, the president every eight years at least. Sometimes it's only four years, sometimes it's eight years, depending on the president and the elections. But the people they bring into the government, you see the names coming up again and again and again. People who were in the Clinton administration in the 1990s are showing up in the Biden administration in some of the top level spots. And the bureaucrats stay there all the time. So bureaucrats who came to work in the Clinton administration are still working in their positions in the Biden administration. And they want to maintain their power and maintain their elite status. And I think that's one of the reasons we see the persistence of the secrecy. Anytime a technologically advanced civilization contacts a more primitive civilization, that primitive civilization ceases to exist. Not necessarily through contact or warfare, warfare or conquest, the mere contact. And there's a really great example from anthropological history where anthropologists want to study a primitive tribe. And the tribe have an underlying sociological structure, where if you needed a stone axe, you had to go through a ritual or a series of steps to get permission from the leaders of that society to use the stone axe. The anthropologist brought a number of steel axes with him and to induce people to talk with him about their society and thing like that. He'd give them a steel axe. He has now undercut one of the underpinnings of that society in an unintentionally, but he radically altered that society by the introduction of a steel axe, which is a far superior device than a stone axe. And I think they understand this, that the mere presence of this technology would radically alter our civilizations around the world. And if they introduce any of that technology to us, it's going to change the power structure. I think they fear that. I think the only way we're going to get disclosure is if the alien creatures decide it's time to let us know, and they land somewhere where the government can't cover it up, and the news media is there to interview them. Of course, we have to be careful we don't end up with To Serve Man, which was a wonderful Twilight episode. But the point simply is, I think that the way things are right now, there is no motivation by those in power to reveal the information they have about UFOs. We have hints about how extraordinary this information is, because we can see it in the way they bungled the investigation, but the information we've been able to gather since then that shows where their mistakes were made, and how they were manipulating the data to keep us all in the dark. It starts essentially in the United States with Roswell, and it continues on from that. We had the Robertson panel in 1953, which was CIA sponsored, and the conclusions were obviously written before the guys went to the work of studying the flying saucers and said, well, there's nothing to it, we need to just stop the mystique of flying saucers. We saw the same thing with the Condon Committee, which I think the skeptics still hold up as, here's a scientific investigation of UFOs, and yet the conclusions were written before the investigation began. An Air Force officer wrote to the Condon Committee and said, here's what we'd like you to find. And they wrote back and said, yeah, we can do that. I've got the letter, a copy of the letter up on my blog at www.kevinrandall.blogspot.com. Just type in Hippler letter and it'll take you to that information.

00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:53.540
<v SPEAKER_1>I'll put the blog address in the, I'm just making a note of that now for the listeners, because I tend to forget those in the description of the podcast as well, folks. And listen, on that, Kevin, time will tell. Hopefully, we do get that progress. But I've said on the podcast before, I completely understand folks who have been involved in this subject for a lot longer than myself and others who listen that why they are trepidatious and why they've seen this kind of thing happen before. So hopefully, we get something different, but I can see both points of view. Let's get to some listener questions, Kevin, in the time we have remaining. The first question is from Matt and Matt wants to know, did the egg-shaped objects at Levelland give off any noises?

00:56:53.540 --> 00:57:10.100
<v SPEAKER_2>There was discussion of people hearing sounds, yes. It wasn't universal, but they did hear roars, they did hear sounds, yes. There was noise given off, not only the electromagnetic effects, but also noise and of course, the very bright lights.

00:57:10.100 --> 00:57:15.720
<v SPEAKER_1>Was the noise reported as mechanical? Like you said, a roar, would it be something more mechanical or?

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:25.840
<v SPEAKER_2>No, it was more of a roar, more of, I think you're more lined with a jet engine type thing. But it was that kind of thing, and not everybody reported sound.

00:57:25.840 --> 00:58:01.440
<v SPEAKER_1>And I think as well that you mentioned earlier about perception and at the time that people knew about flying saucers from the headlines in early science fiction, but it would have still been very much one's interpretation of what they were hearing or what they were seeing. Not like now because we've got 75 years of stories and books, and we've got Hollywood movies that we can interpret these things a little bit easier. So it still could have been something maybe a bit more, because a jet engine type noise or a roar isn't normally what we associate, is it with a UFO or a UAP? You tend to associate low hums or vibrations.

00:58:01.440 --> 00:59:33.580
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, I think that it really, again, I think it's the person and how they describe the sounds. And oftentimes the sound is sort of a secondary effect. They're just not really listening. They're looking at the thing in awe. You go through the reports of the people carefully and you can find hints of that sort of thing. It's kind of like the sheriff. If you go through an awful lot of the books published after 1957, which mentioned Levelland, and my own books do the same thing. Say the sheriff saw it, but it was just a streak of light in the distance. And now I got into it in depth. And it's not a streak of light in the distance. It's a solid object seen much closer. So I think we have to look at all of that sort of thing. Sometimes it's the interpretation of the witnesses. Sometimes it's the interpretation of the writers who are reporting on the story. Sometimes it's the government changing things around a little bit. And the writers do that too. They assume things based on what they're reading. Put their own interpretations on it. Sometimes if those interpretations are wrong, the next guy who's reading it puts that same interpretation on it because he read it in another book. And that's why I do something called Chasing Footnotes periodically. You read something about a book and there's a footnote. So you look at, well, what's that footnote saying? You go to that source, which may lead you to another source, which leads you to another source, which finally leads you to the original source. And the original source story is not quite the same as been reported in the latest book. So you have to look at all of those sorts of things as well.

00:59:33.580 --> 00:59:57.380
<v SPEAKER_1>It's a really interesting point. I think many of us, like you say, would take that at face value and say, yep, this is what's written. There's a footnote and like you say, a little bit of digging and you find out that's potentially changed. So interesting point. Mike has a question. During your research into the 1964 Lonnie Zamora incident that occurred near Sirocco, New Mexico, did you hear anything about Lonnie Zamora having a telepathic experience?

00:59:57.380 --> 00:59:58.540
<v SPEAKER_3>No.

00:59:58.540 --> 00:59:59.120
<v SPEAKER_1>Nothing on that?

00:59:59.120 --> 01:01:00.360
<v SPEAKER_2>No, I don't think there's anything to that. I did hear that he was supposedly a drunk, but that's not true either. I think that was another way of belittling the sighting. Lonnie Zamora was a very quiet religious man. And I think this experience frightened him. The Socorro experience frightened him. But in the book I did on Socorro, we look at in depth some of the explanations for it. We find out that something else is important. It's not a single witness case. There are other witnesses that witnessed aspects of the case, either just before Zamora's sighting or hours afterwards. Sometimes it's just a roar in the sky or a blue flame in the sky, and others it's something much better. But you have to look at the whole area in April of 1964, that short period of time around the sighting, and you get some very good information about what he had seen. And of course, there was a big roar associated with that craft.

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:14.620
<v SPEAKER_1>Next question is from Ryan, and Ryan wants to know, could the genetic bottleneck that occurred roughly 70,000 years ago have been due to extraterrestrial intervention? Do you think hybridization could be a goal of such intervention?

01:01:14.620 --> 01:01:53.020
<v SPEAKER_2>I really don't know. I haven't studied, I did study anthropology, and I understand how some of these things work, but I just haven't looked at that in depth. That's one of the many, many side facets of the UFO field, like crop circles and cattle mutilations and abductions and all the crash retrievals. And I've spent most of my research looking at more of the contemporary sightings and the crash retrievals, and the sightings that have multiple witnesses and multiple chains of evidence as opposed to looking something that's very speculative about what they might have done, or how they might have manipulated the human race at some point.

01:01:53.020 --> 01:02:11.960
<v SPEAKER_1>Fair enough. Next up is Jason out in Los Angeles asks, in your opinion, what would be a legitimate or justifiable reason for any world government to keep the full truth of UFOs from its citizens? What would be a reason that would make you think, yeah, it's best we don't tell the general public about that?

01:02:11.960 --> 01:03:15.560
<v SPEAKER_2>I would have said years ago, it may be a religious aspect that would cause undue panic, or the mere knowledge that there's alien creatures out there that may be on the brink of invading the earth, that would have caused panic. I think that pretty well is untrue. I've talked to a number of theologians about this, and they think it would not affect the religions all that much. And I think that we're all now sophisticated enough to understand that if these things have been around for 75 or 80 years, that the invasion probably isn't coming any time soon. It's done nothing to affect my life, so I'm not really worried about it. I think the motivation, again, that was always a question. What's the motivation for the governments suppressing the information? And I think it just has to do with them maintaining their own power and their fear that if the information gets out, it's going to undermine their power in some way. And they're doing everything they can to maintain their power and their financial positions in the world.

01:03:16.400 --> 01:03:44.740
<v SPEAKER_1>On a similar topic, Gnosis, who is a big contributor to the podcast on the Discord channel as well, says, Kevin clearly believes in a deep state infrastructure that has kept the topic secret over many years. Why have we had no smoking gun whistleblowers or deathbed confessions? Do you think it's because those at the top are still confused by the nature of the phenomenon? Or like you said a little bit there, Kevin, could it just be there are dark aspects to the phenomenon that create a natural justification to keep it secret?

01:03:46.380 --> 01:04:08.440
<v SPEAKER_2>First of all, I would say that there really are no deathbed confessions because a deathbed confession by definition must be the admission of committing a crime at some point in your life. If you're on your deathbed and you start talking about flying saucers, it really isn't a deathbed confession by the legal definition. I always like to throw that out for no good reason whatsoever.

01:04:08.580 --> 01:04:09.660
<v SPEAKER_1>That's fair.

01:04:09.980 --> 01:05:55.620
<v SPEAKER_2>But I think that there have been some interesting things. Edwin Easley, who I mentioned earlier was the Provost Marshall of Walliswold, literally on his deathbed mentioned the creatures to his family. Yet when that information is transmitted into the public arena, you see the skeptics doing everything in their power to belittle it. Well, Randall himself didn't witness him saying that. No, I didn't, but his family did, and this is what they told us. I think the problem is we have not gotten somebody who's really on the inside and can present some kind of evidence for that. We've got a lot of people that have talked about that. Some of the people's credibility is not very great. Others credibility is interesting, but all we have are rumors about it. I think it's that sort of thing that we're dealing with something that is basically unbelievable. You believe in flying sausage, men from other planets coming to earth. That's unbelievable. I think that to take that step over, it requires more than somebody in a deathbed confession environment saying, yes, this is what I saw and what I did. We need something more to be presented. If he said, go to my safe, and there's this metal, if you wad it up in a ball and you let it go, it unfolds itself. Well, then you've got something more than just their word on what happened. So I think that's one of the problems we've run into is, you need something more than just these stories. We've got lots of those stories and some of them are from people who are very credible, and some of them are from people who just really aren't very credible at all.

01:05:55.620 --> 01:06:11.120
<v SPEAKER_1>And final listener question from Dave Smethurst. What are Kevin's views on USOs, so unidentified submerged objects, and the possibility of underwater bases? I believe there was a lot of activity off the coast of Vietnam during that conflict.

01:06:11.120 --> 01:06:55.220
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, I saw none of that during that conflict because I was busy doing other things. But there's another subset of the UFO phenomena I really haven't looked at. I will say the Shegg Harbor case, where this thing was seen to plunge into the ocean, and I think of it as more a forced landing as opposed to a crash, because apparently they repaired the craft and they got out of there some days later. I just really haven't looked at that in-depth, but it makes sense if you have the capability of traveling interstellar distances, you may have capability of hiding your base under the ocean, so it'd be very hard for us to detect or on the dark side of the moon or something like that. But I just really haven't looked into that in-depth at all.

01:06:55.220 --> 01:07:10.980
<v SPEAKER_1>It sounds like you've got plenty of books left in you, so I think there's some food for thought within that though, Kevin, even if you are planning on slowing down, like you say. Listen, Kevin, I just want to ask you one more thing about the book, Levelland, what do you want people to take away from reading this book?

01:07:10.980 --> 01:08:10.400
<v SPEAKER_2>What they ought to take away from is how the government manipulated the data that had we done a proper investigation in 1957, we'd be having a whole different conversation about it. I think the evidence is there for something very, very extracurricular of having have it there. I think it's ranked right below the Roswell case because of the number of witnesses and the possibility of good, solid evidence, the interactions with the environment. I think looking at that and seeing how the government manipulated the information and how they kept us from understanding it. I should point out, this was not a phenomenon related just to Levelland, Texas in 1957. The book also covers the French sightings from 1954, and the South American sightings from 1954. In fact, the very first case of electromagnetic effect is from 1909 and is from England. It covers a whole gambit of this kind of thing, the electromagnetic effects related to UFO sightings.

01:08:12.100 --> 01:08:23.000
<v SPEAKER_1>Kevin, you've mentioned you've got a new book coming out in March, which is going to be on Roswell, or it'll be in time for the 75th anniversary. I think it's due in by March, was that right?

01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:52.420
<v SPEAKER_2>I have a book coming out in March, but it's about Project Moondust. And of course, Levelland is out now, and the Roswell book will be out closer to the anniversary of the sighting in Roswell, which is July of 1947. I think it is the third book in my city's trilogy, going with Roswell to Socorro now to, and with Levelland. So it's all part of my series of the city's trilogy.

01:08:52.420 --> 01:09:11.180
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, I'll have the links in the description of how people can pick up Levelland. I'll have a flying disc press, the links in there as well from. I've got speaker, author, researcher, and creator of the Archive of the Impossible at Rice University, Dr. Geoffrey Cripple. Geoff, welcome to the podcast.

01:09:11.180 --> 01:09:15.280
<v SPEAKER_3>Thanks, Andy. Happy to be here even though it took a long time.

01:09:15.280 --> 01:09:55.060
<v SPEAKER_1>It did. And you have been someone that people have, I don't just say this because it would be BS otherwise, but people have asked to have you on for quite a while. So it's nice to finally get this one over the line as such. If it goes really badly, no one ever has to hear it. I just won't release it, but that's never been the case yet. I'm looking forward to it. And Geoff, there's a whole lot of ground to cover or potentially with you that I feel we're going to have to get you back on at another time to cover various aspects that might be a little bit of a sidebar. But I also want to cover your recent time at the Saul Foundations Inaugural Conference. But I want to start off talking about you, your own background and what's been your journey to getting to this point, discussing quote unquote, the impossible.

01:09:56.580 --> 01:14:13.700
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah, I mean, it's been a long journey. I'm 61 years old and my journey began probably as a child. I was uber pious. I was super pious. I grew up Roman Catholic in a German farm family and small business owner in Nebraska, in the middle of the US. And I became very religious and I wanted to be a monk. And I joined a seminary and trained essentially to become a monk and I fell in love with the comparative study of religion when I was in this Benedictine seminary. And so I went on to graduate school in a secular university, University of Chicago, and became someone who studies religion comparatively. I started out in India. I then switched to California and the American counterculture. And so there's a long journey there. There's a long, I mean, I've done a lot of different things, including medieval Christianity, modern Hinduism, and the modern counterculture. I got interested in what I call the impossible today through looking at the counterculture. I sat down with a lot of people, I talked to a lot of people who told me really incredible stories that I knew didn't happen, but that I knew happened. And I had heard these stories before in the past in historical texts, and we were always trained to think of these as exaggerations, or as power plays, or perhaps even as just lies. And I realized that none of that quite worked. Actually, it didn't work at all. It wasn't just, it didn't quite work. It was clearly not true. And so, I got really interested in the early 2000s in this disconnect between people's extraordinary experiences and how academics or intellectuals simply won't treat them, simply don't think about them. And so, I turned to those pretty intensely, and I wrote a couple books. One was called Mutants and Mystics, which was really on superhero comics and science fiction, and The Paranormal Experiences of Artists and Authors, really, behind those stories. And I wrote a book right before that called Authors of the Impossible, which is really my theory of what the paranormal is about. And the book basically argues that it's very much about language and communication and information and that it's really what creates the stories or the narratives that then later become religion or culture or civilization. And I don't mean that in a reductive way, Andy. I wasn't arguing that the paranormal is simply about language. I was arguing that language itself, there's something paranormal about language, and that when we write about the paranormal, it also writes us in a really weird and eerie way. And so I was trying to get at this sort of deeper question about the paranormal and how it produces a story and how we're all embedded in these stories, whether we know it or not. And that's really what got me into this. The UFO thing, I had to write about the UFO when I wrote about the American counterculture because UFOs are everywhere in the 60s and 70s in the US. And you just can't avoid the topic. But as I got into the paranormal more broadly, it hit me hard that a lot of the experiences people talk about in the UFO experience are not unique to the UFO phenomenon. You find them in your death experiences, you find them in precognitive dreaming, you find them in entity encounters, you find them in legends and folklore and myth and all over the place. And so I approach the UFO phenomenon very much as someone who looks at these things cross-controversially. That's kind of where I am today.

01:14:13.700 --> 01:14:32.660
<v SPEAKER_1>It's a really interesting way to get to this, like you say, from that beginning, looking to go into being a monk and how you can segue into this. Not totally unrelated, obviously, with the religious aspect on things. Had you ever had any sort of either paranormal or UFO experience growing up or through into adulthood?

01:14:32.660 --> 01:17:21.340
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah, everybody asked me that. Yes and no. My professional work and my writing about these things is not dependent on my own experiences. But it relies on them for a kind of sympathy or solidarity. I have to say that. I had one sort of overwhelming paranormal experience in Calcutta in the fall of 1989 that I often talk about. It involved an out-of-body experience while I was sleeping and a state of a sort of subtle energy invasion or abduction, I suppose we'd say now. I didn't have that language then. This was 1989. I wouldn't have spoken of this as an abduction experience, but it had all the marks of one. But it made a lot of sense in the context of the Shakti Tantra and the kind of the religious and cultural tradition I was studying at the time. And that struck me, you know, there was something about the Bengali religious context that made this experience not only possible but plausible. And so that sort of, I suppose, that's kind of one source of my conviction that this has something to do with religion, has something to do with human spirituality. In terms of the UFO, I actually didn't think I had ever had such an experience. And I was sitting, I was watching, it was actually America, America's Got Talent was the show. And I was sitting there with my mother who was visiting from another town and this really bad car commercial came on with this stupid flying saucer on a string. You know, it was clearly a hoax. And my mom turns to me and she says, well, Jeff, do you remember the time we saw one of those? And I was like, no, I have no memory. And she proceeded to tell me this extraordinary story of not only seeing a UFO, but it was a large rectangular craft in the sky over northern Nebraska. And it occurred at night. And she said it was so dramatic that we pulled over. We pulled the car over and all four of us got out and looked at this thing in the sky. And my first reaction was, oh, it had something to do with the Air Force Base in Omaha, which is, you know, not a couple hundred miles from there. She said, no, this was rectangular and it obviously couldn't fly, but there it was in the sky. And so, I mean, obviously, I had an encounter, but I don't remember it.

01:17:21.340 --> 01:17:49.220
<v SPEAKER_1>It wasn't life-defining as such at that moment. And I think it's fair. You mentioned that bias that you don't have just because you've had some sort of experience, and that's completely fair. I use my own couple of sightings as such that I would never call experiences, because I think I've spoken to enough individuals who have had true experiences to know that minds were just sightings, but you wouldn't believe them. I don't think I would believe if someone told me some time ago, they saw what I saw.

01:17:49.220 --> 01:17:49.480
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah.

01:17:49.480 --> 01:17:59.040
<v SPEAKER_1>So I think it's unfair for me to judge completely, even though I may be hypocritical in that time, so I'll say, I don't think so. But I think you'd allow that opinion, aren't you?

01:17:59.040 --> 01:19:00.180
<v SPEAKER_3>You know, something, one close mentor of mine is Jacques Vallee, and something he says a lot is, he wouldn't believe any of the data had he not seen a flying saucer in broad daylight over a suburb in Paris in 1955. I mean, it was just like, there it is. It's as clear as the car going down the highway or my neighbor friend. This isn't something I can question. So it's that minor experience that I think creates a kind of sympathy or solidarity. And so when people were telling me these stories in California in the 2000s, I was like, oh yeah, that can happen. You know, I mean, because it happened to me. I was literally out of my body and I had a kind of energetic download and I had the whole shebang. And so I was like, yeah, I think you're telling me the truth.

01:19:00.180 --> 01:19:20.620
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. It's a strange one. And that's, I wonder, is that sort of small experience enough that has that knock on effect, especially someone as dramatic as Jacques Vallee and the impact he has in the UFO topic and UFO community over the decades. Like you say, had he not had that experience, does he ever get involved at all, especially in the way he has?

01:19:22.080 --> 01:21:07.120
<v SPEAKER_3>I don't know. That's a good question. I think there were a couple of things in Jacques' early life that shaped him. One was seeing the flying saucer over the suburb in Paris in Pontoire, but the other was working at the observatory and hearing his fellow scientists mock and make fun of Amy Michel. And Jacques was a correspondent with Amy Michel, and he found their attitudes really arrogant and bizarre. And so I think it was this combination of having such an experience and seeing how the topic was treated by cultural elites that really turned Jacques into who he became. And that in his, you know, his absorption in esoteric literature, I think is deeply informative. I think that's not often talked about here, but he certainly talks about it. He certainly writes about it. So I think that's a big part of this, this, this quote. And that's, that's how I connected to Lele's books. When I first read them as a historian of religions, I was like, oh, here is a man doing the history of religions. I mean, here is someone who clearly understands the history of folklore and mythology and religion and who also clearly understands the physics and the astronomy and the computer science. So to me, it was that combination that was so stunning and kind of blew me away. It wasn't one of the other. I had had a lot of the history of religions. I knew that intimately. I had studied science. I did not have that intimately, but it wasn't one or the other. There was the two together.

01:21:07.120 --> 01:22:08.080
<v SPEAKER_1>I often think it just takes that small action for things to change for people. I like analogies now and again. This might be a good one, might be a terrible one. I'll let you be the judge of that. But if you're sitting on a bus and it's a cold winter's morning, the windows are frosted up. You can't see out the windows and everyone's on the bus. You're all having the same experience. You're all going to somewhere. You may get off sooner or later. However, I'm the guy who likes to roll his sleeve up and polish the window so I can see outside. Now, I'm now having a totally different experience because of what I can see through this little window I've created for myself, whether it's curiosity or nosiness or just habit, I don't know. But everyone else on the bus potentially could be seeing the same thing, but they don't because they choose to look ahead, stare out the window at the front and see the same thing as everyone else. And that just makes me wonder, is it just those small differences or small curiosities that either get folks involved in the impossible, the paranormal, this phenomenon, that maybe stop others not ever getting involved or seeing what others could see?

01:22:08.640 --> 01:22:22.600
<v SPEAKER_3>I think with the paranormal, it's a bit trickier because so many paranormal experiences are negative. They're terrifying. So it's not just that you don't rub your window, it's that you want the shade down.

01:22:22.600 --> 01:22:24.280
<v SPEAKER_1>You don't want to.

01:22:24.280 --> 01:23:55.580
<v SPEAKER_3>So you're not going to let the critter inside the bus. It's sort of like putting the covers over your head as a child or being afraid of going to sleep with the window open, which is what I had when I was a little boy. So I think it's more than that. It's more than a lack of curiosity. There's a genuine fear that people experience. I also think, you know, one of my jokes about paranormal events is that they're trying to get our attention. And what academics and also frankly, what religious people often do is they essentially say, ignore the event. You know, either ignore it because it's crazy and stupid and it's gonna tempt you away from your scientific or rational worldview, which is what I think academics or conventional scientists often think. Or if you're a religious believer, it's going to distract you from the contemplative goal or from the salvific goal of the religion in question. But it's happening and it's trying desperately to call us into some kind of reflection on the story we're caught in. And so I personally think that these things need to be paid attention to. It doesn't mean we need to believe them or bow down to them. I'm not suggesting that, but we need to listen to them and to take them very seriously as nudges or, you know, pushes sometimes.

01:23:56.760 --> 01:24:14.880
<v SPEAKER_1>In that case, a lot of folks tend to these days put the paranormal. And I think if I say the paranormal, you're talking entities, spirits, ghosts. That's what I think of personally. Under the same umbrella as the UFO phenomenon, do you still think there's quite a distinct difference between those experiences?

01:24:14.880 --> 01:25:49.460
<v SPEAKER_3>No, I don't think there's a distinct difference. I mean, I'm with Jacques on this one. And that gets us into trouble, of course, because some people in the UFO world want to suggest it's nuts and bolts. It's a technology. It's a physical craft. And I'm not denying that, but it's also an apparition and a vision. And people who have these encounters often develop extraordinary abilities. They become precognitive, or they become clairvoyant, or they begin to have dreams. So whatever these objects are, they have psychic or spiritual effects on relatively hairless monkeys, i.e. us. And for me, the definition of the paranormal is something that is occurring in the physical environment and the mental world at the same time. It's not one or the other. It's not a hallucination. It's not just a subjective illusion. On the other hand, it's not simply a chair or a wall or a craft out there either. It somehow is participating in both realities. And that just shatters all of our boxes. We don't have a way of thinking about that because we as bodies and brains are splitting up the world constantly into a mental dimension and a material dimension. And my strong suspicion is that's just not what the world is. It's how we split it up, but let's own that. Those are our divisions. Those are not divisions out there in reality.

01:25:49.460 --> 01:25:55.520
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. You mentioned the phrase previously in your books, thinking impossibly.

01:25:55.520 --> 01:25:55.740
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah.

01:25:55.740 --> 01:26:23.060
<v SPEAKER_1>I think when I initially see, when I first started looking at your stuff a couple of years back, you know, archive of the impossible, my immediate thought, and it's not this is through Ripley's Believe It or Not, that type of thing that, you know, it's just kind of weird and wacky things, but it's not, it's just that whole, to many, this is the impossible. There are no UFOs, there are no aliens, there are no ghosts, there are no paranormal, but that's what the impossible is, isn't it? And if you could explain that for us, what is thinking impossibly?

01:26:23.060 --> 01:27:22.060
<v SPEAKER_3>Sure. So what I mean by the impossible is something essentially philosophical. And essentially what I mean is that what is possible or impossible is the function of a particular worldview. It's not a function of reality. You know, things that are impossible in one worldview still happen, but they're just ignored or they're dismissed. And things change from worldview to worldview in a really radical way. I know that as a historian of religions. I mean, things happen in some religious contexts that don't happen in other religious contexts. And so there's something about reality, there's something about possibility and impossibility that's really a function of the human being. It's not a function of reality. Reality itself is impossible all the time, but it's still possible, it still happens. And let me give you an example, because I'm just babbling.

01:27:22.060 --> 01:27:23.500
<v SPEAKER_1>No, no, go on.

01:27:23.500 --> 01:28:57.640
<v SPEAKER_3>Take rocks that fall from the sky. You know, in the 16th century, French peasants were reporting these rocks falling out of the sky and into their field, and often they were on fire. And the scientists in the cities were just like, these farmers are idiots. You know, rocks don't fall from the sky, for God's sake. That's stupid. But of course, the peasants were right. They were absolutely correct about the things that they were witnessing. And what we needed was a new model of outer space to make sense of why in fact, actually rocks do fall from the sky. And, you know, we call the meteorites now. And so what happened there is not that the phenomena changed, but that our model of the universe changed to encompass and keep on the table those things that were always happening, but that the scientific elites were saying were impossible. And that's what I'm saying about all these other things, Andy. I'm saying, look, what we need as a model or a framework to keep all this stuff on the table and not to keep shoving it off the table and telling us silly things like that it doesn't happen or it can't happen. Of course, it happens. Of course, it can happen. It does all the time. Stop saying that. Change your view of what reality is or what the natural world is or how it works so that we can start thinking about these things in a more coherent and humane way.

01:28:57.640 --> 01:29:08.620
<v SPEAKER_1>Those changes throughout history don't normally happen in an instant, usually as a chain reaction or a slow process over time. Is that a phase you think we may be in right now?

01:29:10.820 --> 01:31:42.880
<v SPEAKER_3>I wish. I think we're in a lot of confusion. And I think we're in a lot of trouble actually. And I think a lot of that's because of our religions and not just because of our science. You know, a lot of the resistance to paranormal phenomena is religious resistance. The religious people essentially say this is real, but it's demonic. And that does not help. That does not get us very far. And it also ignores the fact that what every student of religion knows, really from class one, is that the sacred has a negative aspect and a positive aspect. The demon is as numinous as the angel, and the devil is as divine as God is, you know, in the history of religions now. I know that's a heretical thing to say, but in the larger history of religions, it's certainly true that the sacred is not this force or this energy that's always beneficent and helpful. In fact, it's often terrifying, and it will sometimes kill people. It's more like radioactive energy than it is, you know, the angel or the unicorn in your daughter's bedroom. And so I think once we step into a larger historical framework and we recognize that the numinous is more like an energy or a power that is appearing in the environment, then we are much less likely to say, oh, it's negative, therefore, we have to resist it. No, you don't have to resist it. You have to understand it and you have to treat it differently, perhaps, but you don't have to deny it. So that's really what I'm getting at. I worry a lot as a historian of religions about, frankly, religion and the kind of, frankly, the stupidity of our religions, the ignorance of our religions and how they do not deal adequately with these things. They offer us something important. There's a kind of transcendent or vertical dimension to our religions. It's very useful and very powerful, but their widespread condemnation of these phenomena is really destructive, I think, at the end of the day.

01:31:43.040 --> 01:32:17.000
<v SPEAKER_1>I'm not religious in the slightest myself, but I appreciate other people can be to varying degrees. And I've always said in terms of various religions with a limited knowledge of them, they can't all be right, but they can certainly all be wrong. And I just wonder, given the differences between various religions, I know they have similarities at times, depending on the religion to religion, but given the differences, does that just show that we're potentially as a species, not in a place that we can accept, you know, a non-human intelligence being declared or visiting as you know?

01:32:17.000 --> 01:37:08.820
<v SPEAKER_3>Well, okay, so let's go to that. Let's go to the US and let's go to the language of non-human intelligence. And let's go to Congress and, you know, the whistleblowers and everything that's going on today. You know, politicians are people, too, and their voters are religious in all kinds of ways. And military people, it turns out, are people, too. So are scientists. They're all people. And if you talk to people who have run some of these programs, some of these classified programs, they will often tell you that where they really met resistance was in the military or in the government from very religious military leaders or politicians. And they met resistance on religious grounds, not on military grounds or not on intellectual grounds, but on religious grounds. And that's, I suppose, that's what I'm most worried about. And I'll give you a good example. In the US now, the UFO phenomena is generally understood within what I call the alien invasion narrative. There's a mythology that the alien is somehow invading our sacred airspace. And therefore, what we should be about is fighting, fighting this alien force and pushing back on this, quote unquote, threat. Okay? That's extremely dangerous. Because for one thing, I think these non-human intelligences, if we want to call them that, have been with us for thousands of years. If they wanted to kill us, trust me, we would have been food a long time ago. There's no question in my mind about that. So this attitude we have of fighting back or treating these as threats is really a projection of our own violence and our own really ignorance about what's going on, I think. And I think what you see in the States, or see what you see in North America, let's say more broadly, is a gradual move out of that alien invasion narrative into something that I think is much more powerful and much more positive. So if we want to stick to movies, if Independence Day appeared in the 1990s, all about punching out the alien as it were, arrival happened in 2017 or 2016. And if you watch the movie Arrival, the military doesn't do anything at all. It's actually, the military stands back, and it turns out the reason the alien presence is there is to communicate with human beings because we need each other. And they're going to teach us about the circular nature of time, and we're going to do something for them in 3,000 years, which they somehow know about. So, it's a complete break, is what I'm trying to say. And I know it's a movie, Andy. I'm not saying, Arrivals to Truth and Independence Day is not... I'm not saying that. I'm saying, no, these are cultural narratives that appear, and they grab the public in particular ways. And the way the public has been grabbed, at least in the US over the last 60, 70 years, is through the Cold War, frankly. And all we can imagine is this alien. I mean, we use the same word, alien, for an illegal immigrant, as we do a non-human intelligence. And in France, they used the word for someone who was mentally ill. I mean, what does that tell you? So I think there are resources in history to think about this, and we have to be more aware of the cultural narrative or the mythology, as we call it in my field, that is driving the whole thing. And a mythology is not a bad story or a false story. It's a story so deep that we don't know it's a story. It's the water the fish are swimming in. And I think we're in such a story right now. And I think what these paranormal events are trying to do is say, hey, wake up. You're in a story. You're in a story. You're in a movie. Don't you see that? Don't you want to change the story? Do you really like this movie you're in? Because guess what? It ain't working so well for a lot of people. But we're caught in these narratives. And I think what these events are trying to do is essentially shock us out of that.

01:37:10.080 --> 01:38:23.140
<v SPEAKER_1>I think as well, it's a fair point you raise around these experiences. And perception is a big thing. And I wonder, like, if I go into my son's bedroom at night when he's sleeping before I go to bed and tuck him in and accidentally, you know, nudge him and he wakes up out of his dream with a fright. I'm not doing anything malicious there. But how he perceives that is someone's in my room, I've just woken up. I'm scared. My heart starts racing. He sweats and he might cry. But from my point of view, I didn't mean that. I was trying to do something else and it came from a good place. His perception of what's happened could be completely different. And actually, true story, Jeff, just a few days ago, I woke up in the middle of the night. And one of my recurring dreams since childhood, which always terrifies me, is waking up, the door opens and no one's there. And I woke up during the night, looked over at the door, just getting a sense of something was not quite right, thinking I've just woken up, whatever it might be, and the door opens. And my son steps in. But just from the hall light being on in a silhouette, I was terrified. He just wanted to come in, because he'd woken up during the night and needed the toilet, that was it. But perception in these events can be a big thing. And I wonder, is that a sort of what goes on with some of these incidents?

01:38:24.280 --> 01:40:58.980
<v SPEAKER_3>I think that does sometimes go on. I also think it's more than that. Let me give you another example. Take our dog, which you may have heard earlier, her name is Delilah. Well, I take her to the veterinarian, and the poor thing just shakes. She shakes uncontrollably because that veterinarian is going to do things like put her out and take teeth out of her mouth and poke her and do all kinds of things that poor Delilah has no idea what's going on. And so, okay, I intend good by that. I intend to take care of Delilah, but she doesn't know that. Yeah. Yeah. On the other hand, I eat meat. You know, what is a cow's relationship to a human being? We, I grew up taking care of cattle, as it were, but it was so that they could be slaughtered, and we could eat hamburgers and steaks. So are human beings not demonic vis a vis cows? It sure looks like it. If I was a cow, I would be very distrustful of human beings. And so these non-human intelligences we talk about as demons, well, maybe they are demonic in some sense. Maybe their intentions are not ours. Fine. But our intentions are not those of the other species we interact with either. So I guess I'm just trying to get us to expand our sense of what a value and morality. And I also can tell you, again, as a historian of religions, that the word demon, it just comes from the Greek daimon. And the daimon is not this evil being. The thing with horns and tail and all that stuff you get out of Christian myth, it all comes from a Christian daimonization of the god Panpon, you know? It's essentially a cultural war that the Christians won and the Greeks and the Romans lost. And okay, well, once you know that, it's like, well, come on. I mean, why are you still talking about daimons as if somehow these are obviously true? No, that's just a cultural war that people won, you know, thousands of years ago. And you're just beating up on other people right now.

01:40:58.980 --> 01:41:31.500
<v SPEAKER_1>Interesting. It just makes me think how the common depiction of Santa Claus is Coca-Cola. So, you know, that's, and that's it. That's what people know, but, you know, great market employee. Some of that expanding of thinking like you're talking about, people are trying to do this through various different ways. Folks like yourself have got conferences going on. And before we talk about the Seoul Conference, let's mention your own events that were hosted in 2022 and 2023 under your Archive of the Impossible banner. Can you talk about those and what those aim to achieve?

01:41:31.500 --> 01:44:14.200
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah, I can. I'd love to talk about those. You know, when I was at the Seoul Foundation at Stanford, it reminded me a lot of our own conferences at Rice, by the way. But but in a very different sense, we. I decided to hold these two conferences in 2022 and 2023 to sort of announce our archival project to the world. We we have about 15 collections now in this archive, well over a million documents. They were actually started by Jacques Vallee, who approached me in about 2014, asking for help to place his files and correspondence in a university archive. I went back to my own institution and say, hey, can we do this? And it turns out we could. And so we initiated this with Jacques, but then a whole bunch of other researchers then essentially gave us their life work. And I felt that this was a really important thing to do in the humanities and the social sciences and the sciences. And so to hold these conferences, when we did that, it shattered every IT model that the university had. The response was incredible, Andy. Just to give you an example, when we host a lecture, we typically get about 500 views for the entire life of the lecture. We had 150,000 views in two weeks. You know, it was just like, it was like global and it was overwhelming. And our IT people were just like, oh my God, what is going on? And these were huge events. This is about 200 people came at each of these events. And I hosted about 30 different academics and experiencers, really from around the world, from Argentina to France to Canada, to the US., to the UK. And my experience of it was that intellectuals very much want to do this. They very much want to talk about these things, but they generally lack a context or a community of other intellectuals, historians, philosophers, anthropologists, you name it, doing it. And so what these conferences do is they provide that context. They provide that community and people come in from all over the world, either physically or online, and they can watch these addresses and take part in this conversation.

01:44:16.320 --> 01:44:18.840
<v SPEAKER_1>Have you got plans to host another in 2024?

01:44:20.280 --> 01:45:14.880
<v SPEAKER_3>So I'm on leave at the moment, sabbatical. I spent four years at a dean's office. I'm exhausted, to be honest. I've been dealing with other people's problems for four years, and I'm just fricking tired. So there won't be one in 2024. I do hope to have one in 2025, and then to keep going. I should add that they're very expensive, Andy. So I have to raise money every time I do one of these. And when I say very expensive, I mean, there are three or four times what it costs to hold a regular conference, because there's just so many people coming. There's so much food involved. There's so much travel. There's so much room and board. So I have to be careful about the money, to be really practical about it.

01:45:14.880 --> 01:46:13.720
<v SPEAKER_1>No, that's completely fair. And I think that's something that Dr. Gary Nolan and Dr. Peter Scaffish, who we'll go on to talk about now as well, will attest to in their own hosting of the Saul Foundation. Now, as we record this, it's early December, Saturday the 2nd of December, to give folks a peek behind the curtain. Dr. Nolan, as of yet, hasn't given the full official green light for the information on the Saul Foundation to be discussed at length. That was something he asked for as a sort of academic handshake beforehand that people who attend don't share things online, keep it private. And as much as possible, once he gives the green light a few weeks after, there will be videos, there'll be discussions and all sorts as well. That's sort of been adhered to, but this won't go out until the green light has been given, which I would expect will be any day now anyway. So we're going to talk about the Saul Foundation, but what was your experience? Obviously you were invited, you were a speaker on the Sunday, I believe. Was that correct?

01:46:13.980 --> 01:46:15.260
<v SPEAKER_3>I spoke on Saturday.

01:46:15.260 --> 01:46:16.500
<v SPEAKER_1>Oh, did you? Okay.

01:46:16.500 --> 01:48:16.460
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah, I was the last speaker to speak formally. And I think Gary and Peter wanted me to talk last, to try to bring things into the present, but also to give a, I don't want to say a spiritual or religious, but a kind of existential meaning to the event. And I said pretty much what I said earlier. I come at this as a historian of religions. Much of the proceedings, as you no doubt know at Stanford, involve the sciences and public policy and politics. And that is very, it's a very different focus than we had at Rice. At Rice, the emphasis was very much on the humanities and the social sciences and on the experiencers themselves, really. So, I felt sitting there, you know, for the whole thing, and I saw the whole thing. I listened to every talk very carefully. I felt that the Rice event on the Archives of the Impossible and the Soul Foundation event were very complimentary. They were essentially two sides of the same coin, or two sides of the same floating sphere, if you want to be flatland about it. And I was frankly thrilled. I felt that the Stanford event was historic in the ways we normally use that word. I felt like the papers and presentations were excellent, but there was something about just holding the event in the medical school at Stanford University that was incredibly significant. And we finally had a voice, as it were. And so I think it was that authorization that was really what will be remembered about that event long after we've forgotten what individuals said.

01:48:16.460 --> 01:49:14.240
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, I think it's... And this is no disrespect to any other events or conferences that happened, but it's one thing renting an expo hall somewhere, a big empty shell, and filling it with stages and booths. But to have a credible facility like that, host an event that was clearly discussing, and let's put it bluntly, UFOs. And the science and the academia around that, it was a big thing. As much as Harvard with Avi Loeb, The New York Times, Leslie Kean, Ralph Blumenthal, Helene Cooper, you're starting to get some big organizational names entertaining the subject. And I think that's something that when I asked you earlier about, are we moving into that new phase? That's my hope that these big names continue to come on board. What were the conversations like for folks like yourself mingling? Because you were literally sitting next to these individuals, names that folks in UFOs will know well, folks that names who are now just becoming more prominent, such as a Carle Nell, what was that like?

01:49:14.240 --> 01:50:31.740
<v SPEAKER_3>Well, I knew a lot of these people, of course, before the event. And so part of it was simply meeting old friends. But again, there was this historical element to it. It was meeting a friend at an event that we knew on some level meant something. And I really want to emphasize that institutional or cultural cache that the organization had. Something similar, by the way, happened at Rice in the years before that, is that when the academics came, they recognized very quickly and clearly that we were treating the subject with a great deal of gravity and that this was a research university, and that all of the powers and all of the money and all of the stuff that a research university represents was being focused on this. And so that felt different as well, by the way. And I think when people have that voice and have that kind of space, they'll often say things that they certainly think, but they might not otherwise say. And I think that's what happened at Stanford, and I know that's what happened at Rice.

01:50:31.740 --> 01:51:26.280
<v SPEAKER_1>Your essay or talk was titled, well, given from what you sent me, shooting down souls, good luck with that, some paradoxical thoughts on the UFO phenomenon from a historian of religions. And I like that because something that came from the essay and what it sounds like is people can talk about this with a smile on their face and acknowledge, do you know what, some of this might sound ridiculous, but to appeal to those who are maybe not in the room yet, but looking through the door, looking through the window with an interest to try and get them in, you know, we're aware of what we're seeing, we're aware of the language being used, we're aware of the connotations, but here is out, let's have a conversation. And those, that multi-disciplined approach, I think is huge in getting this forward. So a lot of what you said within the essay, you've mentioned at the start of the podcast, but what were some of your, what did you want folks to take away from that overall talk?

01:51:26.280 --> 01:53:30.400
<v SPEAKER_3>Well, that the saucer and the soul are very much related. And that when people talk about these things as threats and want to shoot them down, they basically are talking about shooting down souls. And of course, that makes no sense, Andy. That's a category collision there. And that was the point of the joke. And that was kind of the point of the talk is, look, we're talking about things that are clearly related, but you're splitting them up in a way that is probably not truthful to what's going on. You know, I have to say, though, the other participants, that talk was received very well, by the way. And I don't believe for a second that there's any bad will or anything other than good intentions in everyone else. Everyone's trying to do the right thing. And the military and the political people who were there, they're just, they're trying to get this talked about, they're trying to get it on the table. And the truth is, is that the stuff in Congress and the whistleblowers has really helped the conversation. I mean, it really has given us a kind of, I don't want to say cover, but it's given us an authority in the culture that we certainly lacked. Before, you know, it was a kind of eye rolls, and there's always a joke of a tin foil hat or a little green man or something. Not so much anymore. People are like, wait a minute, something showing really is on the radar, and now the government's talk, we just have this bill with all these, this language of non-human intelligence. I mean, what's going on? You know, I mean, the giggle factor is not so funny anymore. And I think that that's really healthy. It's not a conclusion. It's not what we want, but it's better than we had. I'll put it that way.

01:53:30.400 --> 01:54:20.460
<v SPEAKER_1>And another theme within it is that resistance to normalization. You mentioned earlier as well that there's the giggle factor, I think, is a big barrier within that. And just I've always thought in the UK, we are even further behind the US in this conversation still. In the UK, if this story or if it appears in the news, it's a, and finally, and there is the X-Files music, we have the Flying Saucer goes by, the little green man waving at the screen, and there's a laugh and a joke. The same story could be positioned at the beginning of the news, at the top of the hour, really serious music with one of the top journalists presenting, and it has a whole different perception. How do you think we can get to the point where those stories do shift? How do we move it? And even, I don't know if you can talk for the UK, but we seem to really struggle with that here.

01:54:20.460 --> 01:56:56.780
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah. So I think some of that is inevitable. I mean, you know, one of the phrases is thrown around a lot in the US is ontological shock. And that comes from, well, John Mack made it famous in the 90s, but it actually goes back to a theologian named Paul Tillich, believe it or not, in the 1950s. But what it refers to clearly in Mack is this notion that whatever is going on does actually does not feel, cannot be fit into our normal categories. And I do think that to the extent we normalize this or we make it reasonable or frankly scientific, we don't understand it. And that's a really hard message, Andy. People want their world to make sense and they want to know that the world can be fit into their boxes. And what I think a lot of these events tell us is that that's not true. So you know, I think the ontological shock is a problem. I also think, you know, this is Jeff now at 61. I talked to journalists, I talked to media people, I talked to television and film people. And in the back of my head, I'm just thinking, oh, you were, you were my student. And they of course were not my student. But what I'm thinking of is, oh, you're just, you're just another guy or another gal, or you're just another human being. You don't know what the hell you're talking about. And you're talking to me to try to figure it out. And I guess what? I don't know either. So let's just admit we don't know. But that's what people are really bad at admitting, including the media, frankly. The media can be really, really powerful and good, and it can really be stupid and bad. And I've seen both. And I think that's part of the problem. The other issue is people like me now, people in the academy have done a very poor job of communicating to the public about what we do. And I think that has to change. I think we have to grab the public space, and we're going to make mistakes. We're going to probably say things that you probably shouldn't say, things that are wrong. And that's okay. We're still going to get a message out there that I think is more sophisticated and nuanced than we have today.

01:56:58.480 --> 01:58:01.240
<v SPEAKER_1>I think that acceptance that people are still people at the end of the day, like you say, the military, politicians, scientists, academics are still individuals and there's going to be mistakes. And especially in the UFO community, but as well as many others online and social media, the slightest slip up or miscommunication or retelling of the same anecdote, but very slightly different gets jumped on and attacked. And that happens more and more. And I just wonder, the last week especially, Jeff, with politicians taking the stage and discussing UFOs in a big way right now in the US or UAP, I think it's shown that for many who wondered, and I don't know where I stand on this really historically, let's get politics involved in the UFO topic. Let's get them discussing UFOs in legislation. We've seen how much they've muddied the waters just in the space of a couple of weeks, and how heroes have become villains, and villains are starting to become heroes, and something in between is just getting a bit messy now.

01:58:01.240 --> 01:59:59.980
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah. Again, I think the problem always is deeper than we can imagine. Take the political and the military stuff again. I mean, what people want to say is, the disinformation campaigns and the deception, this is all about the military or the politics or the bad actors. But the phenomena itself is deceptive. You know, and that's a function of intelligence, by the way. If you are intelligent, guess what? You're going to camouflage yourself. You're not going to tell your... The person studying the guerrilla tribe is not going to walk into the group of guerrillas and just do his or her thing. They're going to use some camouflage or deception. And I think that's... You know, one of the things I feel very strongly when I... After 20 years of this is that the phenomena itself is deceptive. It ain't what it seems to be, folks. But that's not... And that's not a bad thing necessarily. It just is. And I think once we understand that, we can... Again, we can be... This is what I meant by nuance and sophistication. And I think a lot of the conversation going on in the US. Congress and the US media is frankly naïve. And I just want them... Let me put it bluntly. When NASA puts together a team of intellectuals to study the UFO, I want them to put an anthropologist and a philosopher and a historian on there, too. For God's sake, don't give us just physicists and astronomers. Stop it. Stop. Just stop it. And I don't mean that the astronomers or the physicists don't have something to say. Of course they do. But so do the philosophers and the anthropologists and the historians. You need...

02:00:00.100 --> 02:00:31.840
<v SPEAKER_1>We need to listen to them as well. And that's what I suppose I say the most often. I call it my crabby stage. I mean, I'm in a crabby stage of life, but I really believe that, Andy. I really don't think science is gonna get us there at the end of the day. I think science combined with a historical and kind of humanistic approach will get us a little further, but one or the other, nope, ain't gonna work.

02:00:31.840 --> 02:01:08.100
<v SPEAKER_2>Let's start looking at some listener questions to kind of wrap up and summarize some of the topics we've looked at. So, Jeff, we had David emailed in to say, a spiritual person firmly believes that the spiritual world is divided into increasing levels of attainment, whereby over thousands of years, one's level may rise as our experience, knowledge and kindness and good works grow. Surely it's safe to assume that these non-human intelligences are many thousands of years in advance of us. Because of this huge disparity in advancement, is it safe to assume these entities cannot have any malevolent intent towards us?

02:01:08.100 --> 02:01:55.640
<v SPEAKER_1>I don't think we should assume anything. I mean, again, that notice that the email, I mean, it's like references are reference. There's a third or fourth person there. This model of some species being more advanced than other species, of course, is a post-Darwinian model that we assume. That's again, our myth. That's the mythology we live in and swim in. It may be true. Maybe these non-human intelligences are more highly evolved than us. I don't know, but I just want us to own that story. I want us to reflect on it and be aware that that's a post-Darwin kind of narrative.

02:01:57.280 --> 02:02:33.260
<v SPEAKER_2>A question from Newman. And again, this one's a little bit of a paragraph before we get to the question. So, with many of the explanations brought forward for paranormality sitting right at the edge of our knowledge and understanding of the world, what will it take in Jeffrey's opinion for scientists engaged in quantitative research to accept any evidence of events that exhibit quote-unquote high strangeness and not outright dismiss it as either law of large numbers, selection bias, magical thinking, or just glimpses of another one of Russell's teapots, claims being ultimately due to their obscure nature, unprovable by any study design or instrumentation?

02:02:35.300 --> 02:04:32.220
<v SPEAKER_1>Okay, that's a big question. I think this is where we need the historian of science to step in or the philosopher of science. And I think we need to be more humble about our science. My response to that question is not to say that these are somehow superior forms of knowledge, but these are not scientific forms of knowledge. Science is really good at studying objects and measuring them and predicting their behavior. Whatever is going on in these phenomena also has to do with subjectivity and mind and consciousness. And science is frankly really bad at that. The scientific method does not have a place in it for subjectivity or consciousness. So I just want to say, let's let science be science. Let's let it do what it does really, really well, but let's also acknowledge that some kinds of questions are not scientific questions. So that would be my answer to the email is, again, this is why you need historians and anthropologists and philosophers on the team as well, because they're going to call into question those scientific assumptions about reality. Let me put it this way. Just because science works doesn't mean materialism is true. That doesn't follow at all. But that's the assumption people, I think, often make, that what exists is what science can prove or establish. Well, that's not true. You know, again, scientists are people. They have feelings and emotions, and they love their children, and they have all kinds of things in their lives that cannot be measured, and that have nothing to do with their science. So, I just want to, again, call out our assumptions there.

02:04:33.720 --> 02:04:42.560
<v SPEAKER_2>I like that use of, you know, you can't measure love, but, you know, you still know it's there. So, someone can throw that back at Neil deGrasse Tyson one day, I think.

02:04:42.560 --> 02:05:53.940
<v SPEAKER_1>So, it's not just that, you know. My joke, Andy, is that, for example, a lot of paranormal phenomena involve trauma. And I mean serious trauma. I don't mean a little trauma. I mean big-time trauma. Like near-death experiences, you almost have to freaking die to have a near-death experience. And even then, you might not have a near-death experience. And so, when debunkers say, oh, well, you can't get that in a laboratory, therefore, it doesn't exist, I'm like, no shit. Of course, you can't get it into a laboratory. There's no trauma there. You're not killing anyone. You're not harming anyone. Thank goodness, you know. So don't go to the North Pole and tell me there are no zebras. Say, I'm at the North Pole, and guess what? There are no zebras here, but so what? There are zebras in sub-Sahara Africa. I know that. So it's all about your content. This is where a historian will kick in. It's all about the context again, and what a particular culture or context will allow, or what lives in a particular zone, and what doesn't live in another zone.

02:05:53.940 --> 02:06:02.160
<v SPEAKER_2>I've got a question from Stuart. He's got two questions, actually. The first one is around the Chris Bledsoe family story. How familiar are you? Yeah, you're familiar with it.

02:06:02.160 --> 02:06:03.500
<v SPEAKER_1>I'm very familiar with it.

02:06:03.500 --> 02:06:13.960
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. So what is your opinion of Chris Bledsoe's interpretations of the events surrounding him and his family? Do you believe in the religious interpretation or something else?

02:06:14.000 --> 02:07:01.700
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, I think Chris, you know, of course I know Chris, and I read UFO of God, I know the book. I think Chris is very brave in the book and that he's trying to integrate a pretty dramatic set of UFO experiences into what's essentially a conservative Christian worldview. And I think that's hard to do. So I would say I understand what Chris is doing and I admire it, but I think those those two worldviews are ultimately exclusive of one another is probably what I would say. But that's that says nothing about Chris again, that's not a condemnation or a criticism of Chris at all. It's just a reflection from someone who studies religion, you know, in a kind of comparative fashion.

02:07:01.820 --> 02:07:28.540
<v SPEAKER_2>I've hosted Chris and Ryan on the podcast previously, and I've been very open to see a lot of the or phenomenon I struggle with. I see a lot of posts that I believe are dust on cameras. I see a lot of posts that I believe are satellites in the sky. And I'm not saying that everything is, but I see an overwhelming amount that is, let's just say inconclusive either way, because it's a dot against a black background.

02:07:28.540 --> 02:08:03.180
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. And again, when I read a book, like UFO of God, I'm looking at the religious components, Andy. I'm not looking at whether someone's seeing a satellite or UFO. That's well outside of my expertise, okay? I'm going to believe the satellite expert or the radar expert. I'm not going to believe myself. But when I see somebody using some form of Christianity, or some form of Hinduism, or some form of Buddhism, or whatever it is, to understand their experience, then I feel like I have something to say.

02:08:04.640 --> 02:08:28.100
<v SPEAKER_2>The next one is a scientific question, still from Stuart, and I'm going to bastardize the pronunciation of this, but I'm sure you'll know what I mean when I say it. Do you have any thoughts or insight which can be shared on the thesis that increased connectivity between the caudate potamon, that part of the brain Gary Nolan's discussed in various, leads to a higher propensity for esoteric experience, and if so, why might this be?

02:08:28.100 --> 02:08:52.280
<v SPEAKER_1>Again, I'm not a brain scientist. I'm not a neuroanatomist. I'm not Gary Nolan. I just don't know. And I'm going to listen to what Gary has to say about that. I'm not going to listen to what Jeff Kreipel has to say about that. I just don't know. You know, one of the things you learn when you're a professional intellectual is you stay in your track.

02:08:52.280 --> 02:08:53.380
<v SPEAKER_2>Stay in your lane, yeah.

02:08:53.380 --> 02:09:10.820
<v SPEAKER_1>Stay in your lane. Don't, you know, every time an entertainer gets up at an award ceremony and starts talking about the environment, I'm just like, Oh, come on. I mean, stay in your lane, you know, stay in your lane. And so I'm going to stay in my lane there.

02:09:10.820 --> 02:09:20.200
<v SPEAKER_2>You don't want to be stinging flying around the world to tell us at a conference or award ceremony to stop boiling kettles because it's bad for the environment. I understand that. Yeah.

02:09:20.220 --> 02:09:22.460
<v SPEAKER_1>I'm going to stay in my lane.

02:09:22.460 --> 02:09:39.980
<v SPEAKER_2>Question from Mark. Many people believe that the phenomenon is already here, walking among us on earth. Assuming this is true, how do you think the general population will react? And is there risk of a witch hunt or civil unrest? Or are we unlikely to accept those entities being on us?

02:09:39.980 --> 02:12:04.040
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, so I can speak to that more broadly. I mean, I actually spoke to that at the Soul Foundation, by the way. First of all, one of my jokes is, you know, strange beings come out of the sky and mess with people. That's what we call religion. You know, this thing, this presence, whatever we want to call it, has been here a long, long time, as far back as we can see in human history, actually. So I am not of the school that this is a post-atomic bomb phenomena. I know there are people who believe that and who think that. And maybe it's gotten more cultural relevance in the Cold War, but I doubt very much it's been any more prominent in people's experience, although maybe. I'm willing to be educated there. How people will react to it? You know, even if it's been here throughout human history, it doesn't mean that human cultures that have developed are going to do well. If it shows itself, if there's some kind of disclosure, I actually don't think most cultures and most religions are capable of that. I am more on the negative side of the equation. However, I do understand what people on the positive side are saying. They're essentially saying, religion has this vertical dimension, there's something transcended to the human being, and therefore, it can incorporate some kind of disclosure event in a more efficient way. I get that, I'm fine with that. But I just think, I think whatever this thing is or whatever the nature of this presence is, it's going to mostly violate, mostly not fit in to what our assumptions are and what our beliefs are. And people, Andy, people are very bad at not identifying with their beliefs, to put it mildly. I mean, one of the things I tell my students is, please don't believe your beliefs. Please, please don't think your thoughts. Please step out of your assumptions. But that is so rare and that is so hard for people to do that. But I think that's what this thing will be about if it ever, if it ever really reveals itself in any kind of serious way.

02:12:04.180 --> 02:12:32.260
<v SPEAKER_2>I've had a few folks over the last couple of years, the podcast been going now three and a half years, god nearly four years next year, say to me that if we do get UFOs landing on the White House lawn, is that not the podcast finished? I was like, god no, that's when the questions begin, because that's when all the interesting conversations get to start. So for me, it's like you say, if it ever unveils itself in a true meaningful way, I think there's a whole lot more to understand.

02:12:33.680 --> 02:13:33.980
<v SPEAKER_1>Let me give you an example with the White House lawn comment. First of all, it assumes that the White House lawn is even significant. Yeah. Right? I mean, who says? Who says? I mean, clearly, whatever this phenomenon is doesn't give a wit about our borders or our boundaries or our cultural identities. Not one wit. It's a global phenomenon. It goes on all over the world. It's been going on for as far as we can see back. It simply doesn't care about your nation state or your culture or your religion. And that is, that to me is the spiritual revolution that we have to have. We have to go through that ourselves to see, to recognize what's actually going on here. But again, that requires a kind of comparative or global mindset that I think is fairly rare. At this point.

02:13:33.980 --> 02:13:40.780
<v SPEAKER_2>You mentioned two very different UFO movies earlier, Jeff, Independence Day and Arrival. Have you ever seen District 9?

02:13:40.780 --> 02:13:41.680
<v SPEAKER_1>I have.

02:13:41.820 --> 02:14:17.080
<v SPEAKER_2>I always love the beginning of any alien movie. Certain things that interest me is how they arrive, why they arrive, and like you say, communication aspect, all that kind of stuff. And the idea that it starts off with aliens land over a slum in Johannesburg, South Africa, rather than over Paris or New York or somewhere glamorous and the refugees, while they've got this incredible technology, they're suffering themselves as a civilization and they need help. And I always enjoyed that idea that it was something totally different landing somewhere totally out of the norm, which could very well be the case.

02:14:17.080 --> 02:14:48.860
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. Yeah. I mean, it's been a while since I saw that movie, Andy, but that's one of, again, one of the conclusions I think one reaches if you're sit with this long enough is that it just, it's not restricted to any culture or nation state. It just isn't. I think the reporting is, and clearly the movies are made in particular cultures, but it's clearly the reception is a cultural phenomenon, but the actual phenomenon itself is global.

02:14:48.860 --> 02:15:15.740
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. If I say to you, I'm going to write a script, Jeff, and you're a Hollywood executive, and it's about an interaction between an alien spacecraft and an indigenous tribe somewhere in Northeast Asia. Okay, that's one thing, but okay, let's move that to Chicago, and it's a fight to the death between the last humans, and that's now going to sell. That's got dollars all over it. So I understand those different approaches and what works in Hollywood.

02:15:16.460 --> 02:16:27.300
<v SPEAKER_1>I ran a four-year conference series once on the paranormal and popular culture, and I invited all these Hollywood people to come, special effects people, directors, artists, that kind of thing. And I kept asking them, I'm like, can you make a movie about the paranormal that does not involve spandex or things blowing up? And their answer was no. And I was like, why? We have all these people who know everything about the paranormal. They can help you, but you can't just make basically people punching each other out kind of movies. And their response was, we know what sells. We know what makes money. We don't know if that'll make any money actually. And so we're not going to do it. And I was like, okay, that's an answer. That's an honest answer actually. And now I know, I don't know why the movies are so bad on this. But occasionally you'll see one that's really good on this. I'm the first to admit that. I mean, I thought Arrival was brilliant. But by the way, it's a Canadian director.

02:16:27.480 --> 02:16:28.840
<v SPEAKER_2>Is that why?

02:16:28.840 --> 02:16:39.080
