02:29:30.560 --> 02:30:56.900
<v SPEAKER_3>And people, these days we're talking a lot about paranormal things connected. And I certainly did have paranormal experiences, but not really connected to that. I've also written books from the Catholic viewpoint about demons, demonology, that kind of thing. Had some experiences as part of what happened to me to bring me back into the faith, things that I couldn't explain otherwise. So I certainly have thought about related topics, but this one I sat on, I started doing research maybe 20 years ago, thinking I would write the book, but always written a bunch of books. There was always some other book that was more pressing, would write that. And it wasn't until the piece in 2019 in the New York Times, somehow I missed the one in 2017, I'm not sure how, but I saw that and I said, okay, this is the time to write the book I've always wanted to write. My publisher was a close friend. We were at an editorial retreat, I've been a professional editor too, on the beach at Myrtle Beach, South Carolina, and we're walking out on the beach at night and see some kind of light in the sky. I just said, you're going to think I need a tinfoil hat, but I've always wanted to write a book about extraterrestrial intelligence and the Christian faith. He said, write it, I'll publish it. That gave me encouragement because otherwise I might have had a hard time finding a publisher. But it took a couple of years to write it.

02:30:56.900 --> 02:31:23.880
<v SPEAKER_2>When you mention paranormal experiences and how they're not necessarily related to the UFO topic, I think more and more people are bringing themselves around to potentially there is a connection, a relationship there between the paranormal, just the normal that isn't discovered yet as such or things we don't quite yet understand. Do you think there's anything to that when we talk about the paranormal? What is the paranormal to you?

02:31:23.880 --> 02:34:34.380
<v SPEAKER_3>Well, yeah, there's paranormal and there's paranormal, so it means literally aside the normal, it's just outside on the perimeter of the normal. There's certain things that at least the secular world would call paranormal that as a Christian, I've believed all along, so encounters with demonic powers, even perhaps angelic, good angel kind of experiences. I was once part of, I was in a costal for a while before I was Catholic, and I was in a parish that church that was firmly convinced that it can happen, that God gives gifts to people that are beyond the normal stuff that we have. And of course, the Catholics would affirm that to a lot of saints with all kinds of things that we would now call paranormal, that God can give those gifts. And so they're beyond our nature, but still not the kind of woo that some people would think. For someone like Padre Pio, St. Pio Pietrottino, who his lifetime overlapped my own Italian mystic, what we would call the paranormal was almost normal in his life. It happened so often. So paranormal depends on where you're coming from. From a secular point of view, it often just means something anomalous or outside of the normal. From a Catholic Christian point of view, it would include things that we consider are given by God and therefore not something to puzzle over necessarily, that he can give us gifts that go beyond what we normally have. It would probably include things that are often demonically inspired. And again, I've had encounters with that all kinds. So I've written a couple of books about that. Not about my encounters, but about what the church teaches. And then there are other things that, as a Catholic Christian, I would say we have to be open to. Maybe it is something we just haven't fully understood. The whole thing with remote viewing. A lot of folks just dismiss that as they do. UAP is all demonic. But the kind of thing that some of the saints have done, the people in that parish, that church I was in, the four have done after prayer, being able to see things at a distance, know things inside of people's hearts. You can call that paranormal if you want to. But some people would say ghost and spirit, ghosts are part of the paranormal. Okay, if you want to include that. But again, for Christian, we're convinced that the soul does survive after death, that the body goes into the grave, the soul survives. One day there will be a resurrection, we receive our bodies back again. But that in the meantime, that departed, that human spirit that is no longer connected to its earthly body is what we would call a ghost. And that's all through our traditions in the scripture. It's in all kinds of stories of the saints where they encounter people who have died because God gave them a reason to come back for some reason to interact with the living. So none of that is, you know, once I came to the place of firm Catholic faith, so much of what we consider paranormal was not unbelievable to me.

02:34:34.580 --> 02:35:45.800
<v SPEAKER_2>And again, I'll state, I've said this many times on the podcast and it's not a cop out, but I'm not religious in the slightest. So I've got a very, very broad, generic view of religions and, you know, what I can pick up here and there. And I've always said each to their own. There's none of them, I particularly believe. I've probably got a view that you hear quite often that I imagine is probably in some way wrong, but that most religions are based on something that has a basis thousands of years ago. So they're all very much variations of a theme. I get all religions have their own deities, but some of them overlap and various gods and prophets overlap across religions. But I think generally there's a lot of patterns I can see that make sense, that regardless what religion you believe in, or if you even include your faith in the UFO topic or what you believe in UFOs, the idea that we are connected in some way, that what our soul is, where you go when you die, are you part of a greater consciousness? Is this an experience you have? And it still just seems like a variation on a theme and that UFOs in a way is just a whole other religion itself. Can you talk on that? Where am I right? Where am I wrong?

02:35:45.800 --> 02:39:43.820
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah. Well, it's... Where do I start? I think in one way, one reason that what we would normally call religion, why they have these themes and you see patterns, is that although they're all pretty much asking the same questions and their faith, their religion gives an answer to those questions. So some of the questions would be, how did the universe come to be? How did we come to be? And how did I come to be? Is there a purpose in life? Is there something beyond matter, energy, and force, force fields? Fields. What, you know, what, how do you know right from wrong? Those are all kind of, I used to teach a comparative religion courses in college. And those are the kind of questions that prompt religious answers. And so, so I'd agree they're trying to answer the same questions. I would say if you look at the whole spectrum though, you may have some overlapping experience and certainly in the moral area. But the basic tenets can be really, really different. You know, all the way from the Greek pantheon of Greek gods, none of whom are all powerful or all knowing, but more kind of like elevated humans. To the sense that the Christians and Jews and Muslims would say there's one, one God who created everything out of nothing. That's, you know, it's really different. You do have, and one of the things I used to point my course is that, and pretty much as far as I know, there may be some I don't know about. Although what we call the primal or at least the traditional religious, religious traditions, the older forms of them, including the primal religions, which would be original Native American, indigenous African, and that kind of thing, and European, that you do run into conviction that evil spirits exist. That's one of the things that I began to see, you know, maybe there is something to this evil spirits that they may disagree all these religions on God and all kinds of other things, but it's amazing unless you look at some modern version that's been trying to demythologize, so to speak, the traditional versions of all these religions, I'll say, yeah, there's something out there that's malicious, that's not corporeal, that, you know, can come after us. So plenty of overlap in those things, and again, immorality to a lot of, to a great extent. But the most important issues, I think, tend to be different. So for instance, you know, as a Christian, I would push back some on the idea that's becoming popular in UFO circles, that there's a kind of impersonal consciousness that the universe has arisen from somehow, and that we are all part of that consciousness. I would say, you know, as a Christian, I believe that there is a personal God. He's personal, he's rational intellect, he has a free will, he loves, and that he alone is self-existent, and that everything that is, he created out of nothing, and he created it in love. This is part of what I was talking about in the Soul Foundation Symposium, that he loves us, that we're broken, we need healing, he wants us to be healed. But even when you look at those major things, that still leaves a lot of room for mystery and other kinds of things, other creatures out there like that. But it is different, it's not the notion that we're all part of a great consciousness or that the consciousness is part of the universe. It is that he's a personal God, he's not like the force of Star Wars. He's a personal God, loves, thinks all those things, and he creates out of nothing. So his creation is utterly different from him, and yet he is utterly present to his creation in every way. And because of that, then he's able to do all kinds of things for us, whether it's spiritual gifts that allow us to see things at a distance or to know the future with prophecy. So yeah, I don't know if that's answering your question or not.

02:39:43.820 --> 02:41:41.700
<v SPEAKER_2>No, no, it does. Yeah. And like I say, I don't think anyone could ever come on the podcast and convert me to a religion. I think I'm well past that now at 37 years of age, and no one's ever tried, which is wonderful. Like I say, each to their own, because I know certainly when I go to work and work in an office or entertain anyone on the UFO conversation, it very quickly gets the eye rolls and tuts and little green men conversation. And I imagine anyone of any faith or religion ends up in those same conversations as well. And that's what I think the UFO or religious conversations can be very similar in a lot of ways as well. A few different ways I could take what you said, Paul. I want to bring it back to this first because I think I'm going to come back to some of the things you mentioned there in a short while. So I heard you discuss on another podcast, it might have been about a year ago. I think it was a year ago, Pints with Aquinas. Yes. I'll put the link in the description. It was a three hour conversation, really interesting. It goes back and forward between religion UFOs. I mean, that guy knew what he was talking about. And I'm guessing that was a podcast on religion, but you just happened to be discussing your book. So really interesting conversation. Some of it way over my head, other parts of it really interesting. You mentioned on it, though, Paul, that the UFO topic as a civilization was taken very seriously by us humans, going back around 26 centuries. Ancient writers, ancient philosophers all entertained it. And it only seems to be when the US government got involved in the 1940s, we're talking Roswell, you know, atomic bombs and whatnot, that it took a turn. And now people look down on or dismiss the topic, and there's a real fight to bring that back around. And I wonder, first off, why do you think religion now seems to be something that's so powerful world wide still, as it has been through the centuries, has never had such a knock like that, yet the UFO topic seemed to go the other way so quickly?

02:41:43.180 --> 02:44:12.000
<v SPEAKER_3>Well, I mean, you know, a couple of things. And that is more than half of the book, the first part is about what I call the history of the conversation. So starting with the ancient Greeks and the Romans, before the Christian era, we already had folks talking about these things seriously. And then the early Church Fathers we call them the great writers and thinkers of the first few centuries of the Church. Most of them were very well versed in the Roman and Greek philosophers. And so the questions kind of came to them. And so it is important, yes, to affirm that this conversation was taken very seriously. I say an introduction of my book, if you want to give me a tinfoil hat, we could have to give it to a lot of other people too, everybody from Plato to Aristotle, Aquinas and Augustine, and Benjamin Franklin, and on and on, Thomas Paine. So, but if you look at it, and you can see in the book, the conversation is very lively, all the way through the Middle Ages, all the way through the Renaissance, into the modern era, into the 19th century, the 1800s. And then finally, kind of around 1950, with Sputnik, the beginning of the space age, you had especially among Catholic theologians, a lot of talk about it, because a lot of people were thinking about skies. But it was soon after that, within the next generation of theologians, but not just theologians, scientists as well, no one would talk about it. And we have the documents that have been brought out because of the FOIA, the Freedom of Information Act, demonstrating that the intelligence community and the Department of Defense, it was part of their intention to subject the subject to ridicule, to work with their, what shall I say? It looks like plants. But the folks in the legacy media who will cooperate with them, to turn this into a subject of mockery. And even though they couldn't keep it a secret, there have been plenty of whistleblowers. But most of them were dismissed. That's what they were able to do, is to make them look foolish. So that seems to be what happened with theologians as well as with scientists, that they mostly want to stop talking about it. And that's such a shame. It means that we talk about anomalous phenomena. Well, we are the anomaly, our generation is the anomaly. Historically, the norm was to talk about the subject very seriously and take different positions. Our generation has been the anomaly and it's time we get back to taking it seriously.

02:44:14.400 --> 02:45:29.480
<v SPEAKER_2>Why do you think so many folks world wide? And I would say, generally, there's a far bigger audience that is more comfortable having a conversation about gods, regardless of your religion and prophets, stories from the Bible, the Koran, various different religious texts are far more comfortable with those conversations. And there's far less ridicule than there is about the UFO topic. Why do you think that is? Because again, as somebody who isn't religious, I look and go, and I don't mean to downplay this, but man in the sky, up in the clouds where God isn't in the clouds, I get that. But mythical being up there creates the earth in seven days, however you want to play that Noah's Ark, all these stories that people take so literally. And then you've got the UFO topic, topic where you just have to look at it and go, for someone like me trying to have that conversation with someone else, why couldn't something have evolved ever so slightly before us? And it's a couple of hundred or a couple of thousand years more advanced than it is, if not more. And it came here from some place else, it's been here before, et cetera, and set your own ending. I don't see why the conversations are so different, yet they still seem to be.

02:45:29.480 --> 02:49:07.980
<v SPEAKER_3>Yeah, that's a good question. I think there's several things. One is that, because I've often thought the same thing, the kind of classical Christian imagination, Catholic and otherwise, has room for all the stuff, because we've got room for God, for angels and demons, and so-called paranormal phenomena that we understand is something from God, that you know what happens unless it's demonic. But, so why is that? And the answer I've come to is that, where do I start? First of all, there are a lot of folks who believe that if something's not in scripture, clearly in scripture, that it can't be true, can't be real. And of course, that's easily responded to. I'll say, well, the scripture doesn't talk about dinosaurs or duck-billed platypi or microbes or molecules, but we know they exist. So it's not intended to be an exhaustive encyclopedia of everything that exists. The thing I'm finding is that there's a lot of, I just have to use the word ignorance. I don't mean it in a bad way. It just means lack of knowledge, lack of understanding about the Christian tradition itself. People are totally surprised when they find out that very well-respected figures in the Christian, especially Catholic tradition for centuries, have talked about this seriously and some of them even posit it. Yes, there's intelligent life and if not that, they're at least open about it. They don't seem to understand that's part of our tradition. That's somehow been eclipsed, in part because of the modern intellectual movement, beginning with the Enlightenment, away from anything considered supernatural to it has to be scientific. So that, I guess the church Christians have been almost as much affected in certain ways by the culture of scientism that if science can't explain something, it doesn't exist, as others have. And that's a shame because again, their religion should push back against that. That one of my hopes is that in this conversation, as more and more people who've kind of believed only in what science can show them, begin to realize looking at this topic, there are things out there that can't be measured, that science doesn't seem to have a hold on, that we can say, that's right. Let's talk about that because that's part of what we understand about the world. Don't know if I'm answering your question, but I do think, yeah, part of it was that there was not in scripture the other. It's a very human thing, I find, is that for a lot of people, if something they don't understand something and they don't like it, it makes them uncomfortable, then they just write it off as demons or in some cases, government psyops. There's not a lot of depth and thinking in that regard. It's a reaction. I understand it. I mean, this not everybody has time to read my book with 40 pages and five or 50 footnotes, but it's lamentable, I think, too. I mean, it's the same, like you said, it's the same kind of reaction you get sometimes by scientists. They don't understand it, but they know science doesn't account for it, so they have to write it off as something they do understand. Hallucination, mental illness, aerial phenomena of prosaic nature. And I think religious people tend to sometimes do the same thing. They don't understand it, makes them uncomfortable. It's outside the box. How can I give it a cause that does fit into my religious world? It's an easy out.

